 shift in methodology, but doesn't stop us from doing AI R&D. Let's go with 35.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Technical Infrastructure (The \"Logging Layer\").* Focus on the automatic recording of events.\n        *   Title: Unified High-Risk Logging Framework\n        *   What: A standardized software module integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines to automate event capture.\n        *   How: We will develop a common API that records timestamps, input hashes, and operator IDs across different models.\n        *   Why: It ensures baseline compliance without requiring every research team to reinvent record-keeping.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Governance/Documentation (The \"Technical Documentation\").* Focus on Recital 71's requirement for comprehensive info.\n        *   Title: Living Technical Documentation System\n        *   What: A dynamic repository that links model versions to their training data, validation logs, and risk assessments.\n        *   How: We will implement a version-controlled documentation pipeline using tools like MLflow or DVC to automate the audit trail.\n        *   Why: This protects the institution against regulatory audits by providing an immediate, comprehensive traceability map.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Policy/Risk Assessment (The \"High-Risk Classification\").* Focus on identifying which systems are high-risk to avoid over-engineering everything.\n        *   Title: AI Risk Categorization Protocol\n        *   What: A formal institutional process to determine if a project falls under the High-Risk classification of the EU AI Act.\n        *   How: We will establish a compliance review board that evaluates every new R&D project against Annex III criteria during the design phase.\n        *   Why: It prevents wasteful resource allocation by applying strict logging only where legally mandated.\n\n    *   *Reasoning:* 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   *Number:* 0-100? Yes (35).\n    *   *3 Proposals?* Yes.\n    *   *Distinct/Concrete?* Yes (Infrastruct