e of input/output pairs and timestamps, we turn a regulatory burden into a debugging asset. This ensures that moving from an experimental prototype to a high-risk production system is a configuration change rather than a rewrite.\n\n    *   Is this a major overhaul (100)? No.\n    *   Business as usual (0)? No, the law mandates specific technical capabilities.\n    *   Moderate adjustment? Yes. I'm not changing *what* we research (the AI models), but *how* we build them.\n    *   Score: ~30-40. Let's go with 35 to align slightly with the Researcher, as this is a technical implementation shift. Actually, maybe slightly higher because \"automatic recording\" requires actual engineering work. Let's say 40.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Technical Infrastructure/Tooling (The SDK)*\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Framework\n        *   What: A standardized internal library providing a set of APIs for automatic event logging and metadata capture across all AI projects.\n        *   How: We develop a middleware layer that intercepts model calls to record inputs, outputs, and timestamps without requiring researchers to write custom log code.\n        *   Why: It eliminates redundant engineering effort per project and guarantees a baseline level of regulatory compliance for all high-risk prototypes.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Data/Version Control (The Reference Database)*\n        *   Title: Automated Dataset Versioning and Lineage System\n        *   What: A system to automatically track which specific version of a reference database was used during any given model inference or training run.\n        *   How: Integrating DVC or similar tools into the deployment pipeline to create immutable snapshots tied to each logged event ID.\n        *   Why: This directly satisfies Article 12(3)(b) by providing an unbreakable link between input data and the reference database used.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Process/Validation (The Verification Loop)*\n        *   Title: Human-in-the