s an infrastructure shift. I'd put it at around 30-40. It doesn't change *what* AI we build (the models), but *how* we wrap and deploy them. Let's go with 35.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Infrastructure/Framework.* Create a standardized logging wrapper or middleware that handles Article 12 requirements automatically.\n        *   Title: Standardized Compliance Logging Middleware\n        *   What: A reusable software library integrated into the lab's core AI framework to automate event recording and timestamping.\n        *   How: We will develop a set of API decorators and middleware hooks that intercept inputs and outputs across all high-risk prototypes. This allows developers to enable Article 12 logging with a single configuration flag.\n        *   Why: It prevents compliance overhead from slowing down the prototyping phase for individual researchers.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Documentation/Provenance Tooling.* A tool to link training data, versioning, and validation results (Recital 71).\n        *   Title: Automated Model Provenance Tracking System\n        *   What: An internal tool that automatically links specific model versions to their training datasets, hyperparameters, and validation reports.\n        *   How: We will implement a metadata registry that captures the state of the environment and data lineage during every training run. This system will generate the technical documentation required by Recital 71 on demand.\n        *   Why: It ensures we have an audit trail for technical viability without manual record-keeping.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Monitoring/Risk Detection.* A specific monitoring layer to identify \"risks\" (Article 12(2)(a)).\n        *   Title: Real-time Risk Signal Monitoring Dashboard\n        *   What: A telemetry system designed to detect and flag anomalous behaviors in high-risk systems that might indicate a substantial modification or safety risk.\n        *   How: We will deploy a sidecar monitoring agent that analyzes logs for predefined trigger patterns related to performance drift or error spikes. These alerts will be routed to the risk management team immediately.\n        *   Why: This fulfills the regulatory requirement for identifying situations that may result in high-risk system failures.\n\n    *   Check constraints: 3-5 sentences reasoning? Yes. Number 0-100? Yes. Exactly 3 proposals? Yes. Format (title, what, how, why)? Yes. Word count per proposal (60-110 words)? Let's check.\n    *   Proposal 1: ~70 words. Good.\n    *   Proposal 2: ~75 words. Good.\n    *   Proposal 3: ~75 words. Good.\n    *   No bullet lists inside fields? Yes. Plain prose? Yes. Role maintained? Yes.\n\n    *   Reasoning looks solid.\n    *   Number is reasonable for an engineer (infrastructure change, not scientific pivot).\n    *   Proposals cover three different angles: the \"logging\" mechanism (Article 12(1)), the \"documentation/provenance\" aspect (Recital 71), and the \"risk identification\" utility (Article 12(2)(a)).",
