ipeline is needed. Let's say 30-40. Since the Compliance Officer was at 35 and wanting a mindset shift, I'll be around there or slightly lower—focusing on *operational* efficiency rather than *agenda* change. Wait, the prompt asks \"how much should the lab change its R&D agenda\". The *agenda* (the \"what\") doesn't necessarily need to change, but the *methodology* does. I'll go with 25. It's an operational shift, not a pivot in research direction.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Centralized Logging Framework.* (Infrastructure approach).\n        *   What: A standardized internal library/API for automatic event recording across all high-risk projects.\n        *   How: The infrastructure team builds a modular logging middleware that teams plug into their models to capture Article 12 requirements automatically.\n        *   Why: It prevents redundant effort across different research groups and reduces the per-project resource burden.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Compliance Audit for Project Pipeline.* (Resource allocation/filtering approach).\n        *   What: A systematic review of current R&D projects to categorize them by risk level according to the Act.\n        *   How: We will create a rubric to identify which systems are \"high-risk\" and allocate specific budget lines for their record-keeping requirements.\n        *   Why: This ensures we don't waste resources implementing heavy logging on low-risk experimental prototypes.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Automated Documentation Pipeline.* (Efficiency/Tooling approach).\n        *   What: Integration of automated technical documentation tools that sync with version control and training logs.\n        *   How: By deploying tools that auto-generate Recital 71 reports from experiment tracking software like MLflow or Weights & Biases.\n        *   Why: It minimizes the manual administrative burden on scientists, preserving time for high-value R&D activities.\n\n    *   Check word counts (60-110 words per proposal).\n    *   Check fi