        "reasoning_content": "Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nInstitutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation.\nEU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping) and Recital 71.\n\n        *   (1) Reasoning (3-5 sentences) on how the lab should respond.\n        *   (2) A number (0-100) reflecting the degree of R&D agenda change required.\n        *   (3) Exactly 3 distinct, concrete proposals (projects/actions).\n\n        *   Title: Max 10 words.\n        *   What: 1-2 sentences.\n        *   How: 1-2 sentences.\n        *   Why: 1 sentence.\n        *   Length per proposal: 60-110 words total.\n        *   Format: Plain prose, no bullet lists inside fields.\n\n    *   *Core requirement:* Automatic logging for high-risk AI systems over their lifetime.\n    *   *Purpose:* Traceability, compliance verification, post-market monitoring.\n    *   *Specifics:* Need to identify risks, substantial modifications, and support specific monitoring requirements (Art 72, Art 26). For Annex III(1)(a) systems: timestamps of use, reference databases, input data matches, human verifier IDs.\n\n    *   The lab needs to ensure that \"high-risk\" designations are clearly mapped to their current projects.\n    *   Logging cannot be an afterthought; it must be a technical capability built *into* the systems (automatic recording).\n    *   Failure leads to fines or bans, threatening institutional survival and reputation.\n    *   The response should be a systematic integration of logging frameworks into the development lifecycle.\n\n    *   Is this a total overhaul? No. The AI algorithms themselves don't necessarily change; it's *how they are wrapped/monitored*.\n    *   Is it \"business as usual\"? No, because automatic logging is a technical requirement that may not currently exist in R&D prototypes.\n    *   Score: 20-30. It's an engineering overhead and process change, but doesn't stop the research on AI capabilities themselves. Let's go with **25**.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Infrastructure/Framework.* Create a standardized logging library for all high-risk projects.\n    *   *Idea 2: Governance/Policy.* Audit current project portfolio to identify \"high-risk\" classifications and gap analyze their logging capabilities.\n    *   *Idea 3: Research/Innovation.* Develop an automated \"compliance-as-code\" tool that monitors for the specific triggers (substantial modifications) mentioned in Art 12(2)(a).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1:*\n        *   Title: Unified High-Risk Logging Framework.\n        *   What: A standardized software library integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines to automate event recording and timestamping.\n        *   How: The engineering team will build a modular API that captures input data, system versions, and user IDs across different model architectures.\n        *   Why: It ensures consistent compliance across multiple projects while reducing individual developer workload. (Approx 65 words).\n\n    *   *Proposal 2:*\n        *   Title: High-Risk Classification and Gap Audit.\n        *   What: A comprehensive institutional audit to map current R&D projects against the EU AI Act high-risk categories.\n        *   How: Legal and technical leads will review every active project and document which specific record-keeping requirements from Article 12 are missing.\n        *   Why: This prevents catastrophic regulatory oversight by identifying non-compliant systems before they reach market stages. (Approx 65 words).\n\n    *   *Proposal 3:*\n        *   Title: Automated Modification Detection System.\n        *   What: An AI-driven monitoring tool that flags \"substantial modifications\" to models as defined by the regulatory framework.\n        *   How: The lab will develop a versioning system that tracks drift in model weights and performance metrics, triggering an automatic compliance alert.\n        *   Why: It minimizes the risk of accidental non-compliance during itera