ping Sandbox Policy\",\n      \"what\": \"A formal distinction between Exploratory Research and High-Risk Development to prevent premature logging constraints from stifling early-stage ideas. This creates a tiered approach to record-keeping.\",\n      \"how\": \"We establish a classification system where full Article 12 logging is only triggered once a project reaches a specific technology readiness level or risk profile. Early prototypes remain in a low-friction environment.\",\n      \"why\": \"This protects the intellectual merit of high-risk, high-reward exploration from being stifled by industrial bookkeeping too early.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nKnowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, broader scientific impact.\n\n        *   Recital 71: Purpose is traceability, compliance verification, monitoring. Requires records on characteristics, capabilities, algorithms, data, training/testing, and automatic logging.\n        *   Article 12: High-risk systems must allow automatic recording of events (logs) for risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operational monitoring. Specific requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems (period of use, reference database, input data match, person involved in verification).\n\n        *   Compliance Officer (35/100): Shift to industrial compliance; logging as core engineering requirement; avoid penalties.\n        *   Lab Director (20/100): Infrastructure-level automation; minimize friction for researchers; preserve agility.\n\n    *   As a Researcher focused on the \"knowledge frontier\" and \"intellectual merit,\" my primary concern is that strict, rigid compliance requirements could stifle exploration, introduce overhead that kills creativity, or narrow the scope of research to only those things that are \"easy to log.\"\n    *   However, I recognize that reproducibility—a cornerstone of scientific impact—aligns well with traceability. If we can turn these regulatory burdens into a methodology for better science (e.g., rigorous provenance), it's a win-win.\n    *   I don't want the *agenda* (what we research) to change, but I might support changing *how* we conduct research if it enhances intellectual merit.\n\n    *   Reasoning: We should view these record-keeping requirements not as administrative hurdles, but as an opportunity to institutionalize rigorous reproducibility and transparency in our scientific process. By aligning regulatory \"traceability\" with the academic standard of \"reproducibility,\" we can enhance the intellectual merit of our publications while ensuring compliance. The focus must remain on advancing the frontier; therefore, any implementation should be designed to capture high-fidelity research data without constraining the conceptual scope of our experiments.\n    *   Stance Score: 15/100. (The agenda shouldn't change—we still want to push the frontier—but the methodology needs a slight tweak. It's lower than the Compliance Officer because I care about the *what*, not just the *how*. Lower than the Director because I see it as more than just an infrastructure upgrade; it's a scientific practice).\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Turning logging into a research tool.* (Focus on \"Reproducibility Framework\").\n    *   *Idea 2: Exploring the AI-native way to handle this.* (Researching \"Automated Provenance/Metadata Extraction\" as a new research project). This aligns with my role of pushing the knowledge frontier.\n    *   *Idea 3: A policy for \"Compliance Sandboxes.\"* (Creating areas where experimental, non-high-risk prototypes are exempt from full logging until they transition to high-risk status).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1:*\n        *   Title: The Reproducibility and Provenance Framework.\n        *   What: A standardized protocol for documenting hyperparameters, data lineage, and system versions alongside the required logs.\n        *   How: We integrate a versioned metadata store into our experimentation pipeline that links every model checkpoint to its training telemetry.\n        *   Why: This transforms a regulatory burden into a scientific asset by making every experiment perfectly reproducible. (Word count check: ~60 words).\n\n    *   *Proposal 2:*\n        *   Title: Research on Autonomous Compliance Telemetry.\n        *   What: A new R&D project to develop AI-driven systems that automatically generate comprehensible technical documentation and logs from raw code changes.\n        *   How: We apply LLMs to analyze system evolution and synthesize the \"general characteristics\" required by Recital 71 in real-time.\n        *   Why: It pushes the knowledge frontier of self-documenting AI while solving our compliance overhead problem internally. (Word count check: ~65 words).\n\n    *   *Proposal 3:*\n        *   Title: The Prototyping Sandbox Policy.\n        *   What: A formal distinction between \"Exploratory Research\" and \"High-Risk Development\" to prevent premature logging constraints on early ideas.\n        *   How: We establish a tiered classification system where full Article 12 logging is only triggered once a project reaches a specific TRL or risk profile.\n        *   Why: This protects the intellectual merit of high-risk, high-reward exploration from being stifled by industrial-grade bookkeeping too early. (Word count check: ~68 words).\n\n    *   Role: Researcher? Yes.\n    *   Reasoning 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   Stance score 0-100? Yes (15/100).\n    *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Distinct and concrete? Yes (Standardization, New Research Project, Policy Change).\n    *   Title <= 10 words? Yes.\n    *   What (1-2 sentences)? Yes.\n    *   How (1-2 sentences)? Yes.\n    *   Why (1 sentence)? Yes.\n    *   Length 60-110 words per proposal?\n        *   P1: ~65 words.\n        *   P2: ~70 words.\n        *   P3: ~70 words.\n    *   Plain prose, no bullets inside fields? Yes.",
