sks how much to change the R&D *agenda*. Since this is about record-keeping rather than banning certain types of AI, it's not a major overhaul. But ignoring it is a legal risk.\n    *   Score: 25. (It's an operational shift more than an agenda shift). Wait, maybe 30? Let's go with 30 to acknowledge that we might need to allocate budget away from new research and toward infrastructure.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Tooling.* A centralized logging library.\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Framework\n        *   What: A standardized software library for automated event recording across all high-risk projects. It provides pre-built hooks for timestamps, database references, and user IDs.\n        *   How: We will task the systems engineering team with developing a modular API that integrates with our existing ML pipelines. Researchers will simply import this module to meet Article 12 requirements automatically.\n        *   Why: This prevents every research team from wasting time building their own custom logging solutions.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Process/Governance.* Auditing and Documentation Workflow.\n        *   Title: Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline\n        *   What: A system that synchronizes model versioning with the technical documentation required by Recital 71. It links specific training runs to their corresponding data manifests and risk assessments.\n        *   How: We will implement a CI/CD trigger that generates a compliance report whenever a high-risk model is promoted to testing. This will use metadata from our experiment tracking tools like MLflow or Weights & Biases.\n        *   Why: It reduces the administrative burden on scientists and ensures documentation stays current throughout the system lifetime.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Research/Innovation.* New R&D into \"Explainable Logging.\" (Adding a scientific angle).\n        *   Title: Traceability-Optimized Model Architectures\n        *   What: A research project 