 *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Middleware.\n        *   What: A cross-project software wrapper that automatically captures the required event logs (timestamps, inputs, user IDs).\n        *   How: Develop a Python library that integrates with existing frameworks to pipe metadata into a secure database without manual coding in every model.\n        *   Why: It removes the friction of manual record-keeping from the prototyping phase.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Automated documentation generation (MLOps/Provenance).*\n        *   Title: Auto-Generating Technical Documentation Pipeline.\n        *   What: A system that extracts hyperparameters, dataset versions, and test results to auto-fill the required EU technical documentation.\n        *   How: Integrate tools like MLflow or Weights & Biases with a custom template engine that maps experimental logs to regulatory requirements.\n        *   Why: It ensures documentation is kept up-to-date without diverting engineering time from core R&D.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: A \"High-Risk\" Sandbox/Validation Environment.*\n        *   Title: Compliance-Validated Prototyping Sandbox.\n        *   What: A dedicated deployment environment where high-risk systems are stress-tested specifically for logging failures and traceability gaps.\n        *   How: Create a CI/CD trigger that runs \"traceability audits\" on the system's logs before any prototype is promoted to a pilot phase.\n        *   Why: It prevents late-stage redesigns by catching record-keeping gaps early in the development cycle.\n\n    *   Reasoning 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   Stance number (0-100)? Yes.\n    *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Distinct/Concrete? Yes.\n    *   Fields: title, what, how, why? Yes.\n    *   Field constraints: \"what\" (1-2 sentences), \"how\" (1-2 sentences), \"why\" (1 sentence)? Yes.\n    *   Word count per proposal: 60-110 words?\n        *   P1: title (~4) + what (~25) + how (~25) + why (~15) = ~70. OK.\n        *   P2: title (~5) + w