        "reasoning_content": "Development Engineer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nTechnical viability, speed/quality of prototyping.\nEU AI Act Article 12 and Recital 71 regarding record-keeping (logging) for high-risk AI systems.\n\n        *   (1) Reasoning (3-5 sentences) on how the lab should respond.\n        *   (2) A number (0-100) indicating the level of R&D agenda change.\n        *   (3) Three distinct, concrete proposals/projects.\n            *   Each proposal needs: title (max 10 words), what (1-2 sentences), how (1-2 sentences), why (1 sentence).\n            *   Word count per proposal: 60-110 words total across all fields.\n            *   Format: Plain prose, no bullet lists inside fields.\n            *   Tone/Role: Professional Development Engineer.\n\n    *   Recital 71 & Article 12 focus on *traceability*, *compliance verification*, and *post-market monitoring*.\n    *   Requirement: Automatic recording of events (logs) over the system lifetime.\n    *   Specifics for some systems (Annex III 1a): timestamps, reference databases, input data matches, person IDs.\n\n    *   As a Dev Engineer, my priority is prototyping speed and viability. Manual logging is a bottleneck.\n    *   The response should be to *automate* compliance. We shouldn't let this slow down R&D, but we can't ignore it because \"high-risk\" systems won't get past the legal gate.\n    *   We need standardized infrastructure so that every new prototype inherits these logging capabilities by default without each dev having to implement them from scratch.\n\n    *   Is this a total overhaul of *what* we research? No. We aren't changing our core AI goals (e.g., LLM optimization or Computer Vision).\n    *   Is it \"business as usual\"? No, because logging is often an afterthought in R&D.\n    *   It's a structural adjustment to the *how*, not the *what*. I'd place this around 30-40. Let's go with 35. It requires adding new infrastructure but doesn't change the research direction.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Framework.* Create a standardized logging wrapper.\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Framework.\n        *   What: A middleware layer that automatically captures event timestamps, input hashes, and system states for all high-risk prototypes.\n        *   How: We will build an internal Python library integrated into our CI/CD pipeline to standardize log formats across different models.\n        *   Why: This removes the manual burden from researchers and ensures baseline compliance without slowing down prototyping.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Data Governance/Traceability.* Focus on the \"reference database\" part of Art 12(3).\n        *   Title: Immutable Dataset Versioning System.\n        *   What: A system to cryptographically link specific model versions to the exact state of reference databases used during inference.\n        *   How: We will implement a content-addressable storage system like DVC combined with automatic metadata tagging for every request.\n        *   Why: It satisfies the requirement for identifying reference databases while improving our internal reproducibility metrics.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Monitoring/UI.* Focus on \"post-market monitoring\" and \"identifying risks.\"\n        *   Title: High-Risk Event Telemetry Dashboard.\n        *   What: An observability tool that flags anomalies in logs which indicate potential high-risk failures or substantial modifications.\n        *   How: We will develop a real-time monitoring suite using Prometheus and Grafana tailored to the specific risk metrics defined in Article 79.\n        *   Why: It transforms passive log storage into an active safety tool that accelerates our validation cycles.\n\n    *   (1) Reasoning (3-5 sentences): Check.\n    *   (2) Number (0-100): Check.\n    *   (3) 3 Proposals: Check.\n        *   Titles < 10 words? Yes.\n        *   What (1-2 sentences)? Yes.\n        *   How (1-2 sentences)?