he goal is still AI advancement. It changes the *process* of how we build and deploy.\n    *   Number: 20-30. It's an overhead/compliance cost, but doesn't necessitate stopping current research directions unless they are inherently un-loggable (which is unlikely). Let's go with 25.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Engineering Infrastructure.* Create a standardized logging framework for all high-risk projects to avoid reinventing the wheel and ensure compliance across different teams.\n    *   *Idea 2: Scientific Research into Provenance/Interpretability.* Turn the requirement into a research goal. Develop new methods for \"semantic logging\" that provide more than just timestamps—provide an understanding of *why* a decision was made (intellectual merit).\n    *   *Idea 3: Compliance Auditing Tools.* Build internal automated tools to verify that logs meet Article 12 requirements before the system is pushed to production.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1:*\n        *   Title: Unified High-Risk Traceability Framework\n        *   What: A standardized software library for automatic event logging across all lab projects designated as high-risk.\n        *   How: We will develop a modular API that integrates with our existing training pipelines to capture timestamps, data references, and system states automatically.\n        *   Why: This minimizes the administrative burden on researchers while ensuring seamless compliance with Article 12 requirements.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2:*\n        *   Title: Research into Semantic Provenance for AI Accountability\n        *   What: A research project to move beyond simple event logs toward rich, interpretable records of model decision-making logic.\n        *   How: We will investigate causal tracing and attribution methods to record not just that an event occurred, but the internal weights or neurons that triggered it.\n        *   Why: This advances the knowledge frontier by turning a regulatory burden into a scientific contribution to AI transparency.\n