e throughout the system lifetime.\n        *   Check length: ~65 words. Good.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Annex III Biometric Audit Suite*\n        *   What: A dedicated validation tool specifically for systems involving biometric identification to satisfy Article 12(3).\n        *   How: This project will build specific tracking for reference databases and identity-linking human verifiers into the system's core API.\n        *   Why: It mitigates the highest regulatory risk associated with the most stringent logging requirements of the Act.\n        *   Check length: ~65 words. Good.\n\n    *   Reasoning 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   Number 0-100? Yes (35).\n    *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Distinct/Concrete? Yes (Infra, Doc, Specialized Audit).\n    *   Title max 10 words? Yes.\n    *   What 1-2 sentences? Yes.\n    *   How 1-2 sentences? Yes.\n    *   Why 1 sentence? Yes.\n    *   60-110 words per proposal?\n        *   P1: ~65.\n        *   P2: ~68.\n        *   P3: ~67.\n    *   Plain prose (no bullets inside)? Yes.\n    *   Role maintained? Yes.\n\n    *   Wait, Proposal 3 mentions Article 12(3) which is for Annex III point 1(a). I should make sure the \"What\" describes that clearly.\n    *   Proposal 1: Title \"Unified Logging Framework\". What: A standardized software library integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines to automate event recording. How: The engineering team will develop a middleware layer that captures timestamps, input hashes, and system states across different model architectures. Why: It ensures uniform compliance across diverse projects without requiring every researcher to write their own logging code. (62 words)\n    *   Proposal 2: Title \"Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline\". What: An internal tool that automatically generates and updates the technical documentation required by Recital 71. How: We will implement a system that links Git commits, data versioning tags, and training logs into a living compliance report. Why: 