at kills R&D. But \"business as usual\" (0) is impossible since these are legal mandates for high-risk systems. A moderate adjustment to the *infrastructure* rather than the *research goals* is needed.\n    *   Score: 25/100. (It's an engineering task, not a shift in what we are researching).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Standardized Logging SDK.*\n        *   What: A unified internal library for automatic event logging and metadata capture.\n        *   How: Develop a wrapper around common ML frameworks that automatically captures timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs. It would provide pre-defined templates for Annex III requirements to ensure consistency.\n        *   Why: This reduces the manual overhead for researchers and prevents implementation errors during rapid prototyping.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline.*\n        *   What: A system that generates the required technical documentation from code comments, config files, and experiment logs.\n        *   How: Use CI/CD hooks to scrape hyperparameters, dataset versions, and validation results into a version-controlled living document. This replaces manual report writing with automated artifact collection.\n        *   Why: It ensures the \"up-to-date\" requirement of Recital 71 is met without slowing down development cycles.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Regulatory Stress-Testing Framework.*\n        *   What: A suite of automated tests to verify that logging capabilities actually function as intended under load or failure.\n        *   How: Create \"chaos engineering\" scripts that simulate system faults and then audit the resulting logs for completeness and traceability. This verifies technical viability before a system is deployed.\n        *   Why: It proactively identifies gaps in record-keeping, preventing costly last-minute fixes before market entry.\n\n    *   Check word counts (60-110 words per proposal).\n    *   Check formatting (no bullet lists inside fields).\n    *   Check role consistency (technical viability/prototyping speed).\n\n    *Proposal 1 Check:*\n    Title: Unified Compliance Logging SDK\n    What: A standardized internal library that automates event recording and metadata capture across all projects.\n    How: We will build a framework-agnostic wrapper that automatically logs timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs during runtime. This tool will include pre-configured templates specifically for Annex III requirements.\n    Why: It removes the manual burden of logging from the researchers to maintain high prototyping speed.\n    Word count: ~65 words. Good.\n\n    *Proposal 2 Check:*\n    Title: Automated Documentation Artifact Pipeline\n    What: A system that dynamically generates technical documentation by extracting data from code, configs, and logs.\n    How: We will implement CI/CD hooks that scrape hyperparameters, dataset versions, and validation results into version-controlled documents. This transforms static reporting into a living stream of project metadata.\n    Why: It ensures Recital 71 compliance without requiring developers to manually write tedious reports during rapid iteration.\n    Word count: ~68 words. Good.\n\n    *Proposal 3 Check:*\n    Title: Logging Integrity Validation Suite\n    What: A testing framework designed to verify that the system's recording capabilities are robust and complete.\n    How: We will develop automated scripts that simulate edge cases and failures to ensure the logs capture every critical event accurately. This creates a \"compliance unit test\" for high-risk systems before deployment.\n    Why: It prevents technical failure in record-keeping from becoming a legal liability during post-market monitoring.\n    Word count: ~68 words. Good.\n\n    *Reasoning Check:* 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *Stance Score:* Number 0-100? Yes.",
