anding without sacrificing our primary R&D goals.\n\n    *   Number: The regulation asks for *how* things are recorded (logs), not *what* is being researched (the AI models themselves). It's an engineering overhead, not a shift in research focus.\n    *   Score: 15-20. Let's go with 20 because it requires creating new infrastructure tools and modifying existing workflows, but doesn't change the actual \"science\" being done.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Tooling.* A centralized logging framework.\n        *   Title: Unified Traceability Middleware Framework\n        *   What: A standardized software layer that automatically captures system events, timestamps, and data references across all high-risk AI projects.\n        *   How: We will develop a set of internal API wrappers that researchers integrate into their models to ensure consistent log formatting. This removes the burden of manual record-keeping from individual scientists.\n        *   Why: It maximizes resource efficiency by centralizing compliance logic. (Check length).\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Policy/Audit.* A compliance audit process for new projects.\n        *   Title: High-Risk Classification and Logging Audit Protocol\n        *   What: A mandatory review gate at the project initiation phase to determine if a system falls under Article 12's high-risk categories.\n        *   How: The Lab Director and legal counsel will review project charters to assign specific logging requirements based on Annex III criteria before funding is released. This ensures we don't over-engineer logs for low-risk systems.\n        *   Why: It prevents wasteful resource allocation on unnecessary record-keeping. (Check length).\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Technical/R&D.* Improving the AI's ability to self-document or explain its \"matches.\"\n        *   Title: Automated Reference Database Mapping Tool\n        *   What: A specialized utility for Annex III systems that automatically logs which reference database versions