cover/build. Let's go with 25.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure focus.* Standardize the logging framework across all projects so each team doesn't reinvent it.\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Framework.\n        *   What: A centralized, API-driven software library for automatic event recording across all high-risk AI prototypes.\n        *   How: The infrastructure team will build a modular middleware that captures timestamps and data references by default. Researchers integrate this library into their training and deployment scripts via standardized hooks.\n        *   Why: This prevents every single research project from wasting funding on custom logging solutions.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Process/Governance focus.* Updating the internal R&D lifecycle (the \"paperwork\" part).\n        *   Title: Lifecycle Documentation and Traceability Protocol.\n        *   What: A revised set of institutional standards for technical documentation and versioning of reference databases.\n        *   How: We will implement a mandatory metadata tagging system for all training datasets and validation runs using an internal wiki. This protocol ensures that every model output can be traced back to its specific data version and human reviewer.\n        *   Why: It ensures our scientific ambition is backed by the traceability required for regulatory approval.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: New Research focus.* Turning the regulation into a research opportunity (making compliance efficient).\n        *   Title: Efficient Automated Audit-Trail Research Project.\n        *   What: A targeted R&D project to develop low-overhead methods for recording high-fidelity system logs without degrading performance.\n        *   How: One small team will experiment with compressed logging formats and asynchronous event capturing specifically for high-throughput AI systems. They will benchmark the trade-off between logging granularity and compute resource consumption.\n        *   Why: Opti