ey work (infrastructure) but not necessarily *what* they work on (the research agenda).\n    *   As a researcher, I don't believe we should overhaul our scientific goals just because of logging requirements, but we should incorporate \"traceability\" and \"auditability\" as actual research themes. This isn't a major overhaul of the agenda, but it is more than business as usual.\n    *   Stance: 25/100. (It's a shift in *methodology* and perhaps adding a new sub-field of study, but not changing the fundamental goal of pushing the frontier).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Technical Infrastructure/Tooling.* Focus on automating the logs to keep researchers happy (aligning with Lab Director) but making it scientifically useful.\n        *   Title: Automated Provenance Tracking Framework.\n        *   What: A system that automatically captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, and runtime events into a searchable ledger.\n        *   How: Integrating hooks into our ML training libraries to log metadata without manual entry by the researcher.\n        *   Why: It satisfies Article 12 while improving reproducibility of our scientific results.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Research Project on Interpretability/Traceability.* Turn a mandate into intellectual merit.\n        *   Title: Formal Methods for High-Risk AI Auditability.\n        *   What: A research initiative to develop mathematical proofs and tools that map logs back to specific model behaviors.\n        *   How: Applying formal verification techniques to the event streams generated by our high-risk systems.\n        *   Why: This transforms a regulatory burden into peer-reviewed publications on AI safety and transparency.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Data Privacy/Ethics in Logging.* Address the conflict between logging (recording everything) and privacy/GDPR.\n        *   Title: Privacy-Preserving Event Logging Protocols.\n        *   What: Developing cryptographic methods to ensure logs provide traceability without compromising s