p the development speed high.\n    *   *Stance:* Low change to the *agenda* (what we work on), but moderate change to *tooling*. However, the question asks about the \"R&D agenda.\" The core research goals shouldn't change, just the engineering pipeline. I'll go with 25/100. It's a technical shift, not a scientific one.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Automated Logging Middleware.*\n        *   What: A standardized API wrapper for all high-risk model deployments that captures event logs automatically.\n        *   How: Create a Python library that intercepts inputs and outputs to record timestamps, versions, and metadata into a secure database.\n        *   Why: It removes the manual burden from researchers while guaranteeing compliance with Article 12's automatic recording requirement.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Versioned Data Lineage System.*\n        *   What: An integrated system to track exactly which dataset version and reference database were used for every model inference.\n        *   How: Deploy a tool like DVC or MLflow combined with immutable snapshots of input data linked to specific log IDs.\n        *   Why: This directly addresses the requirement to identify reference databases and input matches for high-risk systems in Annex III.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Compliance-as-Code Template Library.*\n        *   What: A set of pre-configured project boilerplates that include built-in documentation hooks and logging configurations.\n        *   How: Develop a cookiecutter template for new projects that automatically sets up the folder structure and config files required by Recital 71.\n        *   Why: It accelerates prototyping speed by eliminating the need to set up traceability infrastructure from scratch for every new experiment.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1 Check:*\n        *   Title: Automated Traceability Middleware\n        *   What: A standardized API wrapper for high-risk model deployments that captures event logs automatically without manual coding.\n        *   How: We will build a lightweight Python decorator library that intercepts request/response cycles to log timestamps and system states into a centralized database.\n        *   Why: This ensures automatic recording of events while keeping the prototyping process fast and frictionless for engineers. (68 words) - Good.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2 Check:*\n        *   Title: Immutable Data Lineage Pipeline\n        *   What: A system to track and link specific versions of reference databases to individual model inferences in real time.\n        *   How: We will integrate a version-control layer for datasets that assigns a unique hash to every input match, storing these hashes within the event logs.\n        *   Why: This satisfies the specific record-keeping mandates for Annex III systems regarding database references and input data matches. (72 words) - Good.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3 Check:*\n        *   Title: Compliance-Ready Project Boilerplates\n        *   What: Pre-configured development templates that incorporate built-in logging hooks and documentation structures for all new high-risk projects.\n        *   How: The team will create a set of standardized repository skeletons using tools like Cookiecutter to automate the setup of technical documentation folders and log configs.\n        *   Why: It maintains high prototyping speed by removing the repetitive overhead of configuring regulatory infrastructure at the start of every project. (74 words) - Good.\n\n    *   Role: Dev Engineer? Yes.\n    *   Reasoning 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   Stance number 0-100? Yes.\n    *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Distinct/concrete? Yes (Middleware vs Lineage vs Templates).\n    *   Format: title, what, how, why? Yes.\n    *   Constraints: No bullet lists inside fields? Yes. Plain prose? Yes. Word counts 60-110 per proposal? Yes.",
