changing *what* we are researching (AI) but *how* we document it. It's a moderate adjustment. Maybe 40/100? Or lower since the core research goals remain, but the methodology changes. Let's go with 35/100—slightly higher than the director because I want to turn this into actual research projects.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Technical Infrastructure (The \"How\" of logging).*\n        *   Title: Automated Observability Framework for High-Risk Systems\n        *   What: A standardized software layer that automatically captures telemetry and decision logs across all high-risk models.\n        *   How: We will develop a unified API that integrates with our existing training pipelines to log input data, reference databases, and verification timestamps.\n        *   Why: This ensures seamless compliance while providing researchers with high-fidelity data for failure analysis.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Scientific Method/Research (The \"Knowledge Frontier\" part).*\n        *   Title: Research into Log-Based Model Interpretability\n        *   What: A research project dedicated to using the mandatory logs of Article 12 to develop new methods for explaining AI decisions.\n        *   How: We will apply causal inference techniques to the event logs to map how specific inputs lead to high-risk outcomes.\n        *   Why: This transforms a legal requirement into an intellectual contribution toward solving the black-box problem in AI.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Policy/Standardization (The \"Broader Scientific Impact\" part).*\n        *   Title: Open Standard for High-Risk AI Traceability Records\n        *   What: The creation of a public, open-source schema for logging high-risk AI events to be shared with the scientific community.\n        *   How: Our team will collaborate with other EU institutions to define a common data format for \"traceability logs\" that balances privacy with transparency.\n        *   Why: Establishing this standard positions our lab as a leader in responsible