        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"From a development perspective, we must avoid implementing logging as an afterthought to prevent massive technical debt during the transition from prototype to production. The lab should focus on building standardized, modular infrastructure that handles record-keeping transparently, ensuring compliance does not throttle our iteration speed. By automating these requirements into our core tooling, we ensure technical viability without forcing researchers to become administrative clerks.\",\n  \"stance\": 40,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Unified Compliance Logging Middleware\",\n      \"what\": \"A standardized software wrapper for high-risk AI systems that automatically records operational events. It ensures consistent data capture across different prototyping teams regardless of the model architecture.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will develop a shared library that intercepts model inputs and outputs to log timestamps and reference databases automatically. This middleware will be integrated into all base project templates used by the lab.\",\n      \"why\": \"It eliminates manual logging overhead for engineers while ensuring the technical viability of audit trails.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Automated Traceability and Documentation Pipeline\",\n      \"what\": \"A system that automatically transforms experiment metadata and version control history into the technical documentation required by Recital 71. It bridges the gap between raw code and regulatory reports.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will build a pipeline using CI/CD tools to scrape training logs, hyperparameters, and dataset versions from our R&D environment. These outputs will be formatted into standardized, version-controlled Markdown reports.\",\n      \"why\": \"It removes the administrative burden of manual record-keeping that typically slows down prototyping speed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Regulatory Event Simulation and Validation Framework\",\n      \"what\": \"A dedicated testing environment used to verify that automatic logging capabilities correctly identify risk situations as defined in Article 12. It serves as a stress test for our traceability systems.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will create synthetic risk scenarios and adversarial inputs specifically designed to trigger log entries. Engineers will then audit these logs to ensure they provide sufficient detail for post-market monitoring.\",\n      \"why\": \"It guarantees that our logging architecture actually works in practice before we commit to a final deployment.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
