le 12.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will build a library of adversarial edge cases for high-risk systems and quantitatively measure the latency and accuracy of the resulting event logs.\",\n      \"why\": \"This ensures our systems are truly traceable while advancing research on AI reliability, auditing, and the science of observability.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nKnowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, broader scientific impact.\n\n        *   Recital 71: Emphasis on traceability, compliance verification, and post-market monitoring via technical documentation (algorithms, data, training/testing) and automatic event logging.\n        *   Article 12: Mandates automatic recording of events for high-risk AI systems to identify risks, facilitate monitoring, and specifically track usage periods/databases/inputs/personnel for certain Annex III systems.\n\n        *   Compliance Officer (30): Urgent integration, fundamental engineering requirement, avoid penalties.\n        *   Lab Director (40): Infrastructure challenge, centralize capabilities to maintain scientific velocity, make compliance invisible.\n\n        1.  Reasoning (3-5 sentences) on how the lab should respond.\n        2.  Stance score (0-100) regarding changes to the R&D agenda.\n        3.  Three distinct, concrete proposals (Title, What, How, Why).\n            *   Each proposal: 60-110 words total.\n            *   Plain prose (no bullets inside fields).\n\n    *   As a researcher focused on the \"knowledge frontier\" and \"scientific impact,\" I see these regulations as both a constraint and an opportunity.\n    *   Strict logging is a technical overhead, but *traceability* is actually a cornerstone of scientific reproducibility.\n    *   If we treat this just as \"compliance,\" we lose a chance to advance the science of AI interpretability and auditing.\n    *   The goal isn't just to tick boxes for the EU, but to turn these requirements into research contributions (e.g., creating new standards for provenance).\n\n    *   Compliance is necessary, but it shouldn't derail the core research agenda unless we can pivot that agenda to *include* the science of compliance/traceability.\n    *   The change isn't a \"major overhaul\" (100) because we