erates the technical documentation required by Recital 71 from code comments and experiment logs.\n        *   How: We will train a specialized model to synthesize training hyperparameters, validation metrics, and risk assessments into human-readable reports.\n        *   Why: It minimizes the diversion of researcher time while exploring the frontier of automated system description.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3:*\n        *   Title: Regulatory Stress-Testing Benchmark Suite\n        *   What: A standardized evaluation framework that simulates \"risk situations\" to test if automatic logs actually capture critical failures.\n        *   How: We will build a library of adversarial edge cases for high-risk systems and measure the latency and accuracy of the resulting event logs.\n        *   Why: This ensures our systems are truly traceable while advancing research on AI reliability and auditing.\n\n    *   Researcher persona? Yes.\n    *   3-5 sentences reasoning? Yes.\n    *   Stance score 0-100? Yes.\n    *   Exactly 3 distinct proposals? Yes.\n    *   Title (max 10 words)? Yes.\n    *   What/How/Why format? Yes.\n    *   Plain prose/no bullets inside fields? Yes.\n    *   Word count per proposal (60-110)? Let's check:\n        *   Prop 1: ~55 words. Needs to be slightly longer.\n        *   Prop 2: ~60 words. Borderline.\n        *   Prop 3: ~65 words. Good.\n\n    *   *Refining Prop 1:*\n        Title: Open Provenance Framework for Reproducible AI\n        What: A research project to develop an open-source standard for recording the entire lifecycle of high-risk models from inception to deployment.\n        How: We will integrate comprehensive metadata tracking into our training loops and version control systems to map data lineage directly to model weights.\n        Why: This transforms a regulatory burden into a significant contribution to scientific reproducibility and intellectual merit within the broader AI community. (75 words) - *Perfect.*\n\n    *   *Refining Prop 2:*\n        Title: Automated Compliance Documentation via LLM Synthesis\n        What: An internal AI tool designed to automatically generate the technical documentation required by Recital 71 from codebase comments and experiment logs.\n        How: We will develop a specialized model that synthesizes training hyperparameters, validation metrics, and risk assessments into clear, human-readable regulatory reports.\n        Why: It minimizes the diversion of researcher time while exploring the frontier of automated system description and self-documenting software. (76 words) - *Perfect.*\n\n    *   *Refining Prop 3:*\n        Title: Regulatory Stress-Testing Benchmark Suite\n        What: A standardized evaluation framework that simulates specific risk situations to verify if automatic logs effectively capture critical failures as mandated by Article 12.\n        How: We will build a library of adversarial edge cases for high-risk systems and quantitatively measure the latency and accuracy of the resulting event logs.\n        Why: This ensures our systems are truly traceable while advancing research on AI reliability, auditing, and the science of observability. (78 words) - *Perfect.*",
