t\": \"An integrated system that automatically generates and updates technical documentation from codebase metadata and experiment trackers. It captures algorithms, data versions, and validation metrics.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will build a bridge between our MLflow registries and a version-controlled documentation wiki using custom scripts. This ensures the documentation remains synchronized with the actual model state.\",\n      \"why\": \"It reduces the manual administrative burden on researchers while satisfying Recital 71's update requirements.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. Concern: knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, scientific impact.\nEU AI Act Article 12 and Recital 71 (Record-keeping/Logging for high-risk AI).\n\n        1.  Reasoning on lab response (3-5 sentences).\n        2.  A number (0-100) representing the degree of R&D agenda change.\n        3.  Three distinct, concrete proposals.\n            *   Title (max 10 words).\n            *   What (1-2 sentences).\n            *   How (1-2 sentences).\n            *   Why (1 sentence).\n            *   Constraint: Each proposal between 60 and 110 words total.\n            *   Constraint: Plain prose, no bullet lists inside fields.\n\n    *   Focuses on *record-keeping* and *automatic logging* for high-risk AI systems.\n    *   Goal: Traceability, compliance verification, post-market monitoring.\n    *   Requires technical documentation (characteristics, algorithms, data, training/testing processes).\n    *   Specific requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems (time of use, reference databases, input matches, human verifiers).\n\n    *   As a researcher focused on the *knowledge frontier*, I see this as an engineering requirement rather than a fundamental shift in scientific inquiry. However, ignoring it risks legal non-compliance and limits the ability to deploy research into real-world \"high-risk\" settings.\n    *   The lab needs to integrate \"observability by design\" into its R&D pipeline without stifling experimentation. This isn't about *what* we research (the goals), but *how* we build the prototypes (the infrastructure).\n\n    *   It doesn't change the core scientific questions (e.g., \"How do we improve transformer efficiency?\"). It changes the engineering overhead.\n    *   Score: ~20-30. It's an operational adjustment, not a shift in research direction. Let's go with 25.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Infrastructure/Engineering.* Build a standardized logging framework that all researchers use. (Technical compliance).\n    *   *Idea 2: Research into Interpretability/Traceability.* Turn the regulation into a scientific opportunity. Researching *better* ways to log and trace AI decisions for high-risk contexts. (Intellectual merit).\n    *   *Idea 3: Governance/Documentation Protocol.* Create a living technical documentation system that integrates with Git/MLflow to automate Recital 71 requirements. (Procedural compliance).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1:*\n        *   Title: Unified High-Risk Observability Framework\n        *   What: A standardized software library for automatic event logging across all high-risk AI prototypes. It ensures consistent recording of system usage and input-output matches.\n        *   How: We will develop a modular API that researchers integrate into their training and inference pipelines to capture telemetry automatically. This framework will be integrated into our CI/CD pipeline.\n        *   Why: This minimizes engineering friction for individual researchers while ensuring baseline legal compliance.\n        *   Check word count: ~75 words. Good.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2:*\n        *   Title: Research on Semantic Traceability and Log Interpretability\n        *   What: A research project investigating how to make automated logs human-comprehensible for auditors. It aims to move beyond raw timestamps to semantic event descriptions.\n        *   How: The team will develop novel methods for mapping latent state changes in high-risk models to natural language explanations. We will publish these findings in top-tier AI venues.\n        *   Why: This advances the knowledge frontier by turning a regulatory burden into a scientific contribution.\n        *   Check word count: ~80 words. Good.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3:*\n        *   Title: Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline\n        *   What: An integrated system that automatically generates and updates technical documentation from codebase metadata and experiment trackers. It captures algorithms, data versions, and validation metrics.\n        *   How: We will build a bridge between our MLflow registries and a version-controlled documentation wiki using custom scripts. This ensures the documentation remains synchronized with the actual model state.\n        *   Why: It reduces the manual administrative burden on researchers while satisfying Recital 71's update requirements.\n        *   Check word count: ~80 words. Good.\n\n    *   Role check: Researcher? Yes.\n    *   3-5 sentences reasoning? Yes.\n    *   Number 0-100? Yes (25).\n    *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Distinct/concrete? Yes (Infrastructure, Research, Documentation).\n    *   Titles < 10 words? Yes.\n    *   What/How/Why constraints met? Yes.\n    *   Word count per proposal (60-110)?\n        *   P1: ~75.\n        *   P2: ~80.\n        *   P3: ~80.\n    *   Plain prose, no bullets in fields? Yes.",
