ssion, but we should integrate these requirements as new technical challenges. Let's go with 20/100. It's more about the *process* than the *agenda*, but some research effort will now be diverted to \"compliance-by-design\" methods.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Infrastructure for reproducibility.* Turn logging into a tool for scientific rigor.\n        *   Title: Automated Reproducibility and Provenance Framework.\n        *   What: A system that captures every hyperparameter, data version, and training event automatically.\n        *   How: Build an integrated middleware that wraps around PyTorch/TensorFlow to log inputs and outputs in real-time.\n        *   Why: It satisfies the law while solving the \"reproducibility crisis\" in AI research.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Research into \"Compact Logging\".* If we have to log everything, how do we do it without killing performance? (This is a research problem).\n        *   Title: High-Efficiency Telemetry for Large Scale Models.\n        *   What: Research into lossy but semantically preserved logging to reduce storage overhead of Article 12 requirements.\n        *   How: Develop algorithms that summarize system states and event logs using embeddings rather than raw text.\n        *   Why: It transforms a regulatory burden into a novel research contribution in system efficiency.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Formal Verification/Audit tools.* Creating the tools to check those logs.\n        *   Title: Automated Compliance Audit Tooling.\n        *   What: A set of diagnostic tools that automatically scan logs to detect risks defined in Article 79(1).\n        *   How: Implement an AI-driven monitor that flags anomalies in the system's automatic records against a safety baseline.\n        *   Why: It ensures we can prove compliance effortlessly and advances the state of AI monitoring research.\n\n    *   Reasoning: 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   Stance score: 0-100? Yes (20).\n    *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Title length (<10 words)?