al/engineering overhead than a pivot of research direction. However, if we were doing stealthy, non-traceable things, that changes. I'll put it at 20. It's not business as usual (0), but it's far from a major overhaul (100).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Engineering Infrastructure.* We need a standardized logging framework across all projects.\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Framework.\n        *   What: A standardized software library for automatic event recording across all high-risk AI models.\n        *   How: The engineering team will develop an API that captures timestamps, input hashes, and system state changes automatically. This will be integrated into the base CI/CD pipeline.\n        *   Why: It ensures consistent traceability without requiring individual researchers to manually implement logging for every project.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Documentation/Process.* We need a way to maintain technical documentation as requested by Recital 71.\n        *   Title: Dynamic Technical Documentation System.\n        *   What: A live, version-controlled repository for technical documentation that evolves with the AI system's lifecycle.\n        *   How: We will implement a tool linking GitHub commits and model weights directly to a compliance dashboard using automated metadata extraction. This removes the reliance on static PDF reports.\n        *   Why: It fulfills the requirement for up-to-date documentation while reducing the administrative burden on scientists.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Specific High-Risk/Annex III focus.* If we do biometric stuff, we need specific controls (Article 12(3)). Let's create a specialized audit module.\n        *   Title: Biometric Traceability Audit Module.\n        *   What: A dedicated verification system specifically for AI systems falling under Annex III, point 1(a).\n        *   How: We will build an interface that explicitly logs the identity of the human verifier and the specific reference database used during each mat