&D often neglects rigorous operational logging in favor of performance metrics.\n    *   Adjustment: Moderate. We need to change *how* we develop systems and perhaps dedicate some R&D resources to \"traceability research.\"\n    *   Score: 30-40 range. Let's go with 35. It's more about the *process* than the *agenda*, but since the prompt asks how much the *R&D agenda* should change, adding \"compliance engineering\" as a research goal is a shift.\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Technical Infrastructure (Engineering/Ops).* Focus on building a standardized logging framework for all high-risk models.\n    *   *Idea 2: Scientific Research (Knowledge Frontier).* Researching how to make logs \"comprehensible\" or \"interpretable\" without leaking sensitive data or harming performance (addressing Recital 71's \"comprehensible information\").\n    *   *Idea 3: Policy/Governance Integration.* Creating a formal technical documentation pipeline that automatically syncs R&D experiments with the required EU technical documentation.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1:*\n        *   Title: Automated Traceability Framework for High-Risk Models\n        *   What: A standardized software layer integrated into our training pipelines to automate event logging and versioning.\n        *   How: We will implement a middleware that captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, and runtime events into an immutable ledger.\n        *   Why: This ensures all research outputs are compliant by design without adding manual overhead to scientists.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2:*\n        *   Title: Research on Privacy-Preserving Traceability Logs\n        *   What: A research project exploring how to maintain detailed regulatory logs while protecting the privacy of input data.\n        *   How: The team will investigate differential privacy and secure multi-party computation applied specifically to system event logging.\n        *   Why: This pushes the scientific frontier by solving the tension between transparency requirement