cal AI) doesn't change; only the engineering standards do. Let's say 30 to reflect a significant shift in operational overhead.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Technical Implementation of Logging.*\n        *   Title: Automated Compliance Logging Framework\n        *   What: An internal software library that standardizes event logging across all high-risk projects.\n        *   How: We will develop modular API wrappers that automatically capture timestamps, input data, and system state changes. These logs will be stored in immutable databases to ensure audit integrity.\n        *   Why: This ensures we meet the technical mandates of Article 12 without reinventing logging for every new project.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Documentation/Process.*\n        *   Title: Technical Documentation Lifecycle Management System\n        *   What: A centralized repository and workflow for maintaining \"living\" technical documentation as required by Recital 71.\n        *   How: We will implement a version-controlled documentation pipeline integrated with our Git repositories to track changes in algorithms and data. This system will trigger updates whenever a substantial modification occurs.\n        *   Why: It mitigates the risk of regulatory failure during audits by ensuring documentation remains current throughout the AI's lifetime.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Specialized Biometric/Annex III focus (if applicable) or Risk Monitoring.* Let's go with an \"Audit Simulation\" approach to test survival.\n        *   Title: Regulatory Stress-Testing and Audit Simulations\n        *   What: A periodic internal audit program designed to simulate EU regulatory inspections of our high-risk systems.\n        *   How: We will appoint a rotating team to act as regulators who attempt to trace a specific AI decision back to its training data and logs. This will expose gaps in our traceability before actual authorities do.\n        *   Why: It protects the lab's reputation by identifying compliance failures 