        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"To maintain prototyping speed, we must treat these record-keeping mandates as non-functional requirements integrated into our shared infrastructure rather than manual tasks for developers. If every researcher has to hand-code logging logic for Article 12 compliance, our development velocity will plummet. The lab should build automated tooling that captures provenance and event logs transparently in the background. This ensures technical viability without introducing friction into the iterative R&D cycle.\",\n  \"stance\": 25,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Unified Compliance Logging Framework\",\n      \"what\": \"A standardized software library integrated into our core AI stack to automate event recording and timestamping. It provides a consistent API for capturing session data across all high-risk projects.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will develop middleware or decorators that automatically log input hashes, reference database versions, and start/end times without requiring custom code per model. This library will output logs in a regulation-compliant structured format.\",\n      \"why\": \"It preserves prototyping speed by removing the burden of manually implementing Article 12 requirements for every new iteration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Auto-Doc Provenance Pipeline\",\n      \"what\": \"An automated system that extracts metadata from training scripts and version control to generate the technical documentation required by Recital 71. It bridges the gap between code and regulatory reports.\",\n      \"how\": \"The pipeline will scrape hyperparameters, dataset hashes, and validation results directly from our experiment tracking tools into a structured template. This ensures that documentation stays synchronized with the actual software state in real time.\",\n      \"why\": \"This minimizes the administrative overhead of maintaining up-to-date technical records throughout the lifetime of an AI syst