   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Framework (UCLF)\n        *   What: A standardized software module integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines to automate event recording.\n        *   How: The engineering team will develop a middleware API that captures timestamps, input references, and user IDs by default.\n        *   Why: This ensures baseline compliance across all projects without burdening individual researchers with repetitive manual logging tasks.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Policy/Governance.* Establish a review process to categorize systems as \"high-risk\" early on.\n        *   Title: High-Risk Classification and Audit Protocol\n        *   What: A formal internal gating process to identify if a project falls under high-risk categories like Annex III.\n        *   How: We will implement mandatory compliance checklists at the project initiation phase and conduct quarterly audits of technical documentation.\n        *   Why: Early identification prevents the catastrophic cost of retrofitting logging capabilities into a nearly completed system.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: R&D Project on \"Explainability/Traceability\".* Use this as an opportunity to lead in \"Compliance AI.\"\n        *   Title: Automated Traceability and Post-Market Monitoring Tooling\n        *   What: An R&D project focused on creating dashboards that translate raw logs into human-readable compliance reports.\n        *   How: Researchers will develop visualization tools that map system events directly to Article 12 requirements for regulatory inspectors.\n        *   Why: This transforms a legal burden into a market advantage by positioning the lab as a leader in transparent AI.\n\n    *   Check word counts.\n    *   Check constraints (no bullets inside fields, plain prose).\n    *   Verify role consistency (Compliance Officer tone).\n\n    *   *Reasoning:* 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   *Number:* 0-100? Yes (25).\n    *   *Proposals:* Exactly 3? Yes. Distinct? Yes (Infrastructure, Governance