e. Let's go with 35. It's more than business as usual, but doesn't require changing the core R&D goals.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: A Standardized Logging Middleware/Framework.* (Technical infrastructure)\n        *   What: An internal library for standardized event logging across all high-risk projects.\n        *   How: Develop a wrapper around existing telemetry tools that automatically captures timestamps, input IDs, and versioning data.\n        *   Why: Reduces per-project engineering overhead and ensures consistency in regulatory reports.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Automated Compliance Audit Tool/Dashboard.* (Verification)\n        *   What: A tool to verify that a prototype's logs meet Article 12 requirements before deployment.\n        *   How: Build a script that scans log schemas for required fields like start/end times and human identifier tags.\n        *   Why: Prevents costly late-stage redesigns by catching logging gaps early in the prototyping phase.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Immutable Provenance Storage System.* (Data Architecture)\n        *   What: A secure, read-only ledger for storing high-risk system logs and reference database snapshots.\n        *   How: Implement a WORM (Write Once Read Many) storage solution integrated into the cloud pipeline to prevent log tampering.\n        *   Why: Ensures the \"traceability\" required by Recital 71 is technically unfalsifiable for external auditors.\n\n    *   Check constraints again.\n    *   Proposal 1 length check:\n        *   Title: Standardized Regulatory Logging Framework (4 words)\n        *   What: A universal internal library that provides a set of standardized logging primitives across all high-risk AI projects. (18 words)\n        *   How: We will build a lightweight wrapper around our existing telemetry stack to automatically capture system events and metadata. (19 words)\n        *   Why: This eliminates the need for every research team to reinvent logging, maintaining our prototyping speed. (16 