nt doesn't require a complete rewrite of the system architecture.\n    *   (2) Stance (0-100): Moderate change. Not a total overhaul of *what* we research, but a significant shift in *how* we engineer it. Let's say 40/100.\n\n    *   Proposal 1: A centralized logging SDK/Middleware.\n        *   Title: Universal Compliance Logging Framework\n        *   What: A standardized library for all high-risk projects to handle event logging and metadata capture automatically.\n        *   How: We develop a wrapper API that integrates with our existing ML pipelines to log timestamps, inputs, and version IDs.\n        *   Why: This decouples regulatory requirements from core algorithm development to maintain prototyping speed.\n\n    *   Proposal 2: Automated Documentation Generator (connecting code/data to technical docs).\n        *   Title: Live Technical Documentation Pipeline\n        *   What: A system that automatically generates the technical documentation required by Recital 71 from version control and configuration files.\n        *   How: We implement CI/CD triggers that extract model hyperparameters, dataset hashes, and training logs into a structured compliance report.\n        *   Why: It removes the manual burden of record-keeping while ensuring the documentation remains up to date.\n\n    *   Proposal 3: A \"Compliance Sandbox\" or Validation Suite for Logs.\n        *   Title: Log Integrity and Stress Test Suite\n        *   What: An automated testing suite to verify that high-risk systems are actually recording the required events under various failure modes.\n        *   How: We create synthetic workloads that trigger specific risk scenarios to validate that the logging system captures the necessary Article 12 data.\n        *   Why: This ensures technical viability and prevents deployment delays caused by missing or corrupted logs.\n\n    *   Check word counts for proposals (60-110 words).\n    *   Ensure no bullet lists inside fields.\n    *   Maintain rol