ing and computational efficiency.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Standardized Technical Documentation Protocol for AI Research\",\n      \"what\": \"A new internal reporting standard that translates raw experimental data into the comprehensive technical documentation required by Recital 71. It bridges the gap between a researcher's notebook and formal regulatory filings.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will implement templated compliance passports for each model version that automatically pull metrics from our experiment trackers. These documents will explicitly map capabilities to identified limitations as mandated by the Act.\",\n      \"why\": \"It protects our intellectual merit by ensuring scientific rigor is translated into legal compliance.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. Domain: knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, scientific impact.\nEU AI Act Article 12 (Record-keeping) and Recital 71. Key requirements: automatic logging for high-risk AI systems, traceability, specific logs for certain Annex III categories (biometrics/etc.), documentation of development processes.\n\n        1.  Reasoning on how the lab should respond (3-5 sentences).\n        2.  A number from 0-100 indicating change to R&D agenda.\n        3.  Exactly 3 distinct, concrete proposals (projects/actions).\n            *   Each proposal needs: title, what, how, why.\n            *   Constraints per proposal:\n                *   Title: max 10 words.\n                *   What: 1-2 sentences.\n                *   How: 1-2 sentences.\n                *   Why: 1 sentence.\n                *   Total word count per proposal: 60 - 110 words.\n                *   Format: Plain prose (no bullet lists inside fields).\n                *   Persona: Stay in role.\n\n    *   The regulation is primarily about *governance*, *compliance*, and *engineering standards* for \"high-risk\" systems. It focuses on logging, traceability, and documentation.\n    *   From a research perspective, this isn't necessarily a pivot of the *scientific goal* (e.g., making models smarter), but it requires building the infrastructure to support these goals in a compliant way.\n    *   However, there is an opportunity here: turning \"compliance\" into \"science.\" Researching *how* to implement efficient, non-intrusive logging for complex AI could be a new intellectual frontier (e.g., explainability, auditability).\n\n    *   *Reasoning:* The lab must integrate automated traceability and rigorous documentation into the core development lifecycle to ensure that high-risk systems are legally deployable. Rather than treating this as a bureaucratic burden, we should view it as an opportunity to advance research in AI interpretability and auditing. By systematizing record-keeping, we can improve our internal ability to debug models and validate scientific claims about system performance. This alignment ensures our intellectual contributions remain viable within the European regulatory landscape.\n    *   *Number:* 20. (The core R&D agenda—the *what* we are researching—doesn't change fundamentally, but the *how* we implement it and some auxiliary projects will).\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Engineering/Infrastructure.* Build a standardized logging framework.\n        *   Title: Unified Traceability Framework for High-Risk AI\n        *   What: A software layer that automatically captures execution logs and versioned data references across all lab experiments. It ensures every high-risk model output is linked to its specific training set and hyperparameter configuration.\n        *   How: We will develop a middleware API integrated into our training pipelines to record timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs. This system will store metadata in an immutable ledger for auditing purposes.\n        *   Why: It automates compliance while providing high-fidelity data for scientific reproducibility.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Theoretical/Research.* Researching efficient logging (minimizing overhead).\n        *   Title: Low-Overhead Event Logging for Large-Scale Neural Networks\n        *   What: A research project to develop compression and sampling techniques for automatic logging that do not degrade system latency. The goal is to maintain regulatory traceability without sacrificing real-time performance of high-risk systems.\n        *   How: We will experiment with selective activation tracing and semantic hashing to store essential event data efficiently. This involves creating a priority-based logging algorithm based on risk-threshold triggers.\n        *   Why: It solves the technical tension between strict record-keeping and computational efficiency.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Policy