        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"We must integrate standardized logging and documentation frameworks directly into our development pipeline to ensure high-risk systems are compliant by design. While record-keeping adds overhead, failing to automate these requirements will lead to massive delays during the transition from prototype to production. Our focus should be on creating reusable compliance modules that developers can plug into any new project. This approach prevents manual bookkeeping and protects our overall prototyping velocity.\",\n  \"stance\": 35,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Unified Compliance Logging Framework\",\n      \"what\": \"An internal software library providing standardized hooks for automatic event recording and metadata capture across all high-risk projects. It ensures that every prototype has a baseline level of traceability.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will develop API wrappers that automatically log timestamps, input hashes, and database versions to a secure, immutable ledger. This removes the burden of manual logging from individual researchers during iteration.\",\n      \"why\": \"It ensures consistent legal traceability without sacrificing the speed of rapid prototyping.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Auto-Doc Technical Registry\",\n      \"what\": \"A system that automatically generates and updates technical documentation by pulling data directly from version control and training logs. This streamlines the creation of the records mentioned in Recital 71.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will integrate tools like Sphinx or Doxygen with our CI/CD pipeline to extract model hyperparameters and dataset versions upon every deployment. This replaces the tedious manual drafting of compliance reports.\",\n      \"why\": \"It keeps technical documentation up-to-date in real-time as required by the regulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Traceability Validation Suite\",\n      \"what\": \"A dedicated testing framework to verify that logs are actually capturing the critical events necessary for post-market monitoring. It treats logging as a first-class functional requirement.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will create adversarial test cases designed to trigger specific risk scenarios and then programmatically check if those events were correctly logged and attributed. This validates our record-keeping reliability before external audits occur.\",\n      \"why\": \"It guarantees that our logging capabilities actually meet the legal requirements for risk identification.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
