he intellectual merit of our research through more rigorous empirical validation. This shift ensures that our pursuit of the knowledge frontier remains aligned with legal frameworks without stifling innovation.\n    *   *Score:* 20. (The R&D *agenda*—what we are trying to discover/build—doesn't change much, but the *methodology* of how we build it does. It's an implementation detail rather than a pivot in research goals).\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Technical Infrastructure for Logging.*\n        *   Title: Unified Audit-Logging Framework for High-Risk Systems.\n        *   What: A standardized software layer that automatically captures system events and data lineage across all lab projects.\n        *   How: We will develop a middleware API that intercepts inputs and outputs to create immutable, time-stamped logs according to Article 12 specifications.\n        *   Why: It reduces the individual engineering burden on researchers while ensuring universal compliance across diverse projects.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Theoretical Research into Interpretability/Traceability.*\n        *   Title: Formalizing Traceability Metrics for Complex AI Architectures.\n        *   What: A research project to define mathematical standards for what constitutes a \"meaningful\" log in high-dimensional latent spaces.\n        *   How: We will publish papers on mapping internal system states to human-readable event logs to satisfy the \"comprehensible information\" requirement of Recital 71.\n        *   Why: This advances the scientific frontier of AI interpretability while directly addressing regulatory needs for transparency.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Policy/Process Integration.*\n        *   Title: Integrated Compliance and Research Documentation Pipeline.\n        *   What: An automated workflow that synchronizes technical logs with the required regulatory documentation and risk management systems.\n        *   How: We will implement a version-control system for hyperparameters and datase