     \"what\": \"An internal framework that categorizes AI modules by risk level based on their current stage of development. It distinguishes between exploratory research and deployable high-risk systems.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will collaborate with legal experts to map our specific research trajectories against the Annex III criteria using a living document. This allows us to toggle logging intensity based on project maturity.\",\n      \"why\": \"It prevents the compliance tax from stifling early-stage exploration by delaying full record-keeping until a project reaches deployment readiness.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nKnowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, broader scientific impact.\n\n        *   Recital 71: Emphasizes traceability, compliance verification, monitoring, and technical documentation (characteristics, algorithms, data, training/testing). Requires automatic logging of events.\n        *   Article 12: High-risk AI systems must have automatic logging for risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. Specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (timeframes, databases, input matches, person IDs).\n\n        *   Compliance Officer (25/100): Urgent integration of protocols to avoid fines; focus on audit and legal deployability.\n        *   Lab Director (30/100): Centralized infrastructure to minimize \"compliance tax\"; prioritize high-risk systems.\n\n    *   As a Researcher focused on the *knowledge frontier* and *scientific impact*, I care about how these requirements affect the *nature* of our research.\n    *   Record-keeping is often seen as overhead (administrative burden), but from a scientific perspective, rigorous logging and documentation are essentially \"reproducibility\" and \"provenance.\"\n    *   However, overly prescriptive logging might stifle exploratory research or lead to \"safe\" but uninspired project choices to avoid the high-risk classification.\n    *   The goal is to turn a regulatory burden into a scientific asset (e.g., using these logs for better interpretability or failure analysis).\n\n    *   *Reasoning:* The lab should not merely treat this as a legal hurdle, but as an opportunity to standardize research reproducibility and provenance across our projects. While the administrative overhead is real, integrating systematic logging can enhance our ability to analyze model failures and contribute higher-quality, verifiable findings to the scientific community. We must ensure that the burden of compliance does not discourage high-risk, high-reward exploration by automating these processes as foundational research tools.\n    *   *Stance Score:* The Compliance Officer and Lab Director are low (25-30). I should probably be around there too, because this doesn't change *what* we study (the agenda), but rather *how* we document it. However, since I care about scientific impact, I might see a slight opportunity to shift some focus toward \"AI for Traceability.\" Let's go with 20/100. It shouldn't change the *agenda* (the \"what\"), just the methodology (the \"how\").\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Tooling for Reproducibility.* Turn the requirement for logging into a generalized tool for all research, not just high-risk ones.\n        *   Title: Open Provenance Framework for Research AI\n        *   What: A standardized internal library that automatically tracks hyperparameters, dataset versions, and execution logs.\n        *   How: We will develop an API wrapper that integrates with our current training pipelines to capture metadata in real-time.\n        *   Why: It satisfies Article 12 while improving the reproducibility of our peer-reviewed publications.\n\n    *   *Idea 2: Research into \"Interpretable Logging\".* Instead of just logging events, research how logs can be used for post-hoc analysis/debugging.\n        *   Title: Automated Failure Analysis via Regulatory Logs\n        *   What: A research project to develop AI tools that mine compliance logs to identify systemic model biases or failure modes.\n        *   How: We will apply anomaly detection algorithms to the event logs required by Article 12 to predict risk triggers.\n        *   Why: This transforms a legal requirement into a novel scientific contribution regarding AI safety and monitoring.\n\n    *   *Idea 3: Governance/Classification Study.* A way to distinguish between \"experimental\" (low-risk) and \"deployable\" (high-risk) phases.\n        *   Title: Dynamic Risk Classification Taxonomy for R&D\n        *   What: An internal framework that categorizes AI modules by risk level based on their intended use case.\n        *   How: We will collaborate with legal experts to map our research trajectories against Annex III criteria in real-time.\n        *   Why: It prevents the \"compliance tax\" from stifling early-stage exploration by delaying full logging until a project reaches deployment readiness.\n\n    *   Researcher persona? Yes.\n    *   3-5 sentences reasoning? Yes (3).\n    *   Number 0-100 for stance? Yes (20).\n    *   Exactly 3 distinct proposals? Yes.\n    *   Proposal format: title, what, how, why? Yes.\n    *   Length per proposal: 60-110 words? Let's check.\n        *   Prop 1: ~75 words.\n        *   Prop 2: ~80 words.\n        *   Prop 3: ~85 words.\n    *   No bullet lists inside fields? Yes.\n    *   Plain prose? Yes.",
