        "reasoning_content": "Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nKnowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact.\nEU AI Act - Article 12 (Record-keeping) and Recital 71.\n\n        *   High-risk AI systems must allow automatic logging over their lifetime.\n        *   Logs must enable traceability for risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operational monitoring.\n        *   Specific requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems (biometrics/identification): usage periods, reference databases, input data matches, person IDs.\n\n        *   (1) Reasoning on response (3-5 sentences).\n        *   (2) A number (0-100) representing the degree of R&D agenda change.\n        *   (3) Three distinct, concrete proposals (projects/actions).\n            *   Proposal constraints: title (<10 words), what (1-2 sentences), how (1-2 sentences), why (1 sentence). Total length per proposal: 60-110 words. No bullet lists inside fields.\n\n    *   As a researcher focused on the \"knowledge frontier,\" my primary concern is that compliance doesn't stifle innovation, but also that traceability can actually *enhance* scientific rigor.\n    *   Article 12 is essentially about provenance and auditing. It asks for technical capabilities (logging).\n    *   The lab shouldn't stop its core research, but it must integrate \"traceability by design.\" This isn't a pivot of the R&D goals (e.g., we aren't stopping LLM research to do compliance), but it is a change in *how* we build systems.\n    *   Therefore, the response should be: Integrate these requirements into the engineering pipeline and potentially turn \"traceability\" into a research area itself.\n\n    *   Does this require a major overhaul of *what* the lab works on? No. It requires a change in *how* it documents and architectures its systems.\n    *   Is it business as usual? Not quite, because automatic logging is a specific technical requirement that might not be