        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"The lab must transition from a pure research mindset to a systems-engineering approach that treats traceability as a primary functional requirement. Since non-compliance threatens our legal right to operate in the EU market and risks massive fines, logging cannot be an afterthought. We need to integrate automatic event recording directly into the development lifecycle of every high-risk project. This ensures institutional survival by making auditability a native feature of our AI systems.\",\n  \"stance\": 35,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Unified Compliance Logging Architecture\",\n      \"what\": \"A standardized, modular API designed for all high-risk AI projects to implement automated event recording consistently. This framework will serve as the mandatory backbone for any system entering the deployment phase.\",\n      \"how\": \"Our engineering team will develop a shared software library that captures timestamps, input data matches, and user identifiers across various model architectures. We will mandate its integration into all CI/CD pipelines.\",\n      \"why\": \"This prevents fragmented implementation and ensures we can produce uniform logs for regulatory audits.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Dynamic Lifecycle Documentation Pipeline\",\n      \"what\": \"An automated system that synchronizes technical documentation with version control to maintain a living record of AI development. It will track changes in algorithms, data, and validation processes.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will implement a tool that triggers mandatory documentation updates whenever the training dataset or model architecture is modified in our repositories. This link ensures that the technical file remains current.\",\n      \"why\": \"It satisfies the requirement for up-to-date documentation without stifling the speed of research.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Post-Market Monitoring Simulation Framework\",\n      \"what\": \"A specialized testing environment used to verify that system logs accurately capture risk-triggering events as defined in Article 12. It acts as a stress test for our traceability capabilities.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will create adversarial compliance scenarios where engineers intentionally trigger known failure modes to confirm the system records them correctly. These tests will be run before any external release.\",\n      \"why\": \"This proactively protects our reputation by ensuring we can detect and report failures before regulators do.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\nInstitutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation.\nEU AI Act Article 12 & Recital 71 (Record-keeping/Logging for high-risk AI).\n\n        1.  Reasoning (3-5 sentences) on how the lab should respond.\n        2.  A number (0-100) indicating the degree of R&D agenda change.\n        3.  Exactly 3 distinct, concrete proposals (projects/actions).\n            *   Fields: title (max 10 words), what (1-2 sentences), how (1-2 sentences), why (1 sentence).\n            *   Constraints: No bullet lists inside fields, plain prose, 60-110 words per proposal.\n\n    *   *What is required?* High-risk AI systems must allow automatic recording of events (logs) throughout their lifetime.\n    *   *Specifics:* Traceability for risks, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. Annex III 1(a) systems have stricter requirements (timestamps, reference databases, input data matches, person identification).\n    *   *Implications:* We can't just build the model; we must build a *logging architecture* into the system from day one. Documentation is also key.\n\n    *   The lab needs to shift from \"model-centric\" development to \"system-centric\" development. If our current R&D ignores logging or traceability, we face massive fines and market exclusion in the EU. We must integrate compliance requirements directly into the software engineering lifecycle. Failure to do so threatens institutional survival.\n\n    *   Does this change *what* we are researching (the AI breakthroughs) or *how* we build it?\n    *   It changes the *engineering overhead*. It doesn't necessarily stop us from pursuing a specific AI goal, but it adds significant infrastructure requirements. However, for some projects, these constraints might make them unviable.\n    *   Score: 30-40. It's not an overhaul of the \"what\" (the science), but a necessary shift in \"how\" (the implementation). Let's go with 35.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1: Infrastructure/Engineering.* Build a standardized logging framework.\n        *   Title: Unified Compliance Logging Architecture.\n        *   What: A standardized, modular API for all high-risk AI projects to implement automated event recording.\n        *   How: Engineering teams will develop a shared library that captures timestamps, inputs, and user IDs across different model architectures.\n        *   Why: This prevents fragmented implementation and ensures consistency for regulatory audits.\n\n    *   *Proposal 2: Process/Documentation.* Technical documentation system.\n        *   Title: Dynamic Lifecycle Documentation Pipeline.\n        *   What: An automated system to sync technical documentation with version control and model updates.\n        *   How: We will implement a tool that triggers documentation reviews whenever the training data or algorithm is modified in the repository.\n        *   Why: It satisfies the \"up to date\" requirement of Recital 71 without slowing down research speed.\n\n    *   *Proposal 3: Risk/Audit.* Post-market monitoring simulation.\n        *   Title: Post-Market Monitoring Simulation Framework.\n        *   What: A testing environment designed specifically to verify that logs accurately capture risk-triggering events as defined in Article 12.\n        *   How: We will create \"adversarial compliance\" tests where engineers intentionally trigger risks to see if the system logs them correctly.\n        *   Why: This proactively protects our reputation by ensuring we can detect failures before regulators do.\n\n    *   Reasoning 3-5 sentences? Yes.\n    *   Number 0-100? Yes.\n    *   Exactly 3 proposals? Yes.\n    *   Title < 10 words? Yes.\n    *   What (1-2 s), How (1-2 s), Why (1 s)? Yes.\n    *   60-110 words per proposal?\n        *   P1: ~75 words.\n        *   P2: ~80 words.\n        *   P3: ~85 words.\n    *   Plain prose/no bullets inside fields? Yes.\n    *   