eaucratic hurdle but as an extension of scientific rigor and reproducibility. By automating the collection of development metadata and event logs, we can enhance our ability to perform retrospective analyses on model behavior and failures. This approach allows us to satisfy legal mandates while simultaneously improving the intellectual merit of our published research through superior provenance.\n    *   Stance Score: 20/100. (The *agenda*—what we are researching—doesn't need to change, only *how* we document and implement it. It's a process shift, not a goal shift.)\n\n    *   *Idea 1: Automated Provenance Pipeline.* Focus on the \"technical documentation\" part of Recital 71. Create a system that automatically captures hyperparams, data versions, and training logs into a standardized format.\n    *   *Idea 2: Interpretability-Driven Logging Framework.* Instead of just \"logging for compliance,\" build a framework that logs internal state or attention maps (where feasible) to help identify the risks mentioned in Article 12(2)(a). This turns compliance into an interpretability research project.\n    *   *Idea 3: Open-Source Compliance Standard Contribution.* To maintain scientific impact and influence, the lab should lead a consortium to define what \"appropriate traceability\" looks like for high-risk AI, ensuring the standards don't stifle future R&D.\n\n    *   *Proposal 1 (Provenance):*\n        *   Title: Automated Research Provenance Pipeline\n        *   What: A system that automatically captures all experimental hyperparameters, dataset versions, and training metrics into a version-controlled ledger.\n        *   How: We will integrate MLflow or Weights & Biases with custom hooks to ensure every model artifact is linked to its exact development lineage.\n        *   Why: This streamlines the creation of technical documentation while enhancing research reproducibility for peer review. (Count check: ~65 words)\n\n    *   *Proposal 2 (Interpretability/Logging):