 efficient than per-project solutions.\n\nThe key question from my vantage: Does this make better use of our scarce implementation resources? Yes — centralizing a mandatory compliance requirement into one reusable component is textbook resource efficiency. It also lets us leverage existing infrastructure (existing monitoring, data pipelines) rather than building in silos.\n\nI think this clears the bar. It's a high-promise proposal from my perspective. The main caveat would be scoping it carefully to avoid unbounded complexity, but the core direction is right. I'd approve it with a strong promise score. Maybe 82-85? The \"guarantees by default\" language and the breadth of \"across various AI architectures\" introduce some risk that the actual implementation could be more expensive than planned, but the strategic logic is sound.\n\nLet me settle on:\n- Decision: approve\n- Promise: 83 (strong alignment with my concerns about resource efficiency and infrastructure reuse, slight deduction for scoping risks and the ambitious \"across various architectures\" claim that could balloon cost)\n",
