tes 70-80% of the documentation burden.\n\n**From my specific vantage point (resource efficiency, funding constraints, existing infrastructure):**\nThis is a high-leverage, low-cost proposal. It uses what we already have (MLflow), requires minimal new engineering effort (scripts + template), and directly addresses a compliance obligation that would otherwise consume senior researcher time — the most expensive resource in the lab. The ROI is clear: a few engineer-days of development saves potentially hundreds of researcher-hours per year across all model releases. It also reduces the risk of non-compliance due to human error or procrastination on documentation, which could lead to regulatory penalties (a funding-constraint nightmare).\n\nThe proposal doesn't overreach. It's scoped to what MLflow can reasonably provide. It doesn't promise to solve the entire Article 12 logging requirement (which would require system-architecture changes), but it addresses a real and significant slice of the compliance burden efficiently.\n\n**Decision**: This clears my bar comfortably. It's resource-efficient, leverages existing infrastructure, has modest implementation cost, and protects researcher time for scientific work. I approve.\n\n**Promise score**: From my vantage point, this is highly promising. The cost-benefit ratio is excellent, it uses existing tools, the scope is well-defined and achievable, and the payoff (freed researcher time + reduced compliance risk) directly serves the lab's funding-constrained environment. I'd give it around 82-85. It's not a 90+ because it doesn't fully solve Article 12's logging requirements (that needs system-level changes), and the PDF template approach might need iteration for different use cases. But from pure resource-efficiency standpoint, it's very strong. I'll go with 83.\n",
