tomatic event recording, periods of use, reference databases, input data matching, person identification) are NOT addressed at all. This is a documentation tool, not a logging system.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The CI/CD integration part could be prototyped relatively quickly - maybe 2-4 weeks for a basic version that generates a markdown/PDF doc on each merge.\n- However, the \"extract from code\" part is where it gets tricky. If our codebase uses standard config patterns, it's doable. If we have heterogeneous training scripts (which is common in R&D), the extraction will be unreliable and require constant maintenance.\n- The promise of \"eliminating manual effort\" is overstated. Recital 71 documentation includes qualitative descriptions of capabilities, limitations, risk management systems - these can't be auto-extracted from code. Someone still needs to write those sections.\n- As a prototype, a basic version could show value in reducing the tedium of keeping parameter tables and validation metrics updated. But calling it a \"pipeline\" that solves the Recital 71 documentation problem is overpromising.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a pure dev engineering standpoint:\n- The core idea (auto-generate parts of technical docs from structured data in code/repo) has merit as an incremental improvement to reduce manual toil.\n- But it's technically incomplete relative to what Recital 71 actually requires, and the \"extract from code\" framing is somewhat naive about how ML projects are actually structured in R&D.\n- It doesn't address Article 12's actual logging requirements at all (which are the operative legal obligations - the recital provides context).\n- As a prototyping effort: you could build a useful MVP in a sprint or two for the CI/CD doc generation part, but the \"extraction\" layer would likely consume disproportionate time relative to value.\n- The risk is building something that looks compliant on paper (auto-generated parameter tables) while mi