ject implementation might suffice.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal says researchers will \"integrate into their training and inference pipelines.\" This implies working with existing codebases, which is good for infrastructure utilization.\n- However, building a new library means adding maintenance burden to the engineering team. Who maintains this after initial development? This is an ongoing cost.\n- A standardized format also helps with audit readiness — when Article 12 audits come, having consistent logs across projects reduces the compliance overhead significantly.\n\n**Risks/Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The proposal commits \"the engineering team\" to this without specifying scope or timeline. If it becomes a bottomless pit of feature requests from each project, it could consume disproportionate resources.\n- \"Guarantees the technical ability to satisfy Article 12 audits\" is strong language — but does the library alone guarantee compliance? The content of logs (what events are captured) still needs to be determined per system based on its risk profile and Annex III category. A one-size-fits-all framework might not capture all required data for every use case, or it might over-capture, creating data storage costs.\n- The proposal is reasonably scoped: timestamps, system events, modular API. It's not asking to build the entire compliance system, just the logging layer.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a sensible infrastructure investment that reduces per-project compliance overhead. From a resource-efficiency standpoint, building once and reusing is clearly better than building N times for N projects. The main risk is scope creep and maintenance burden on engineering. But as described — a modular API for automatic event recording with consistent formats — it's proportionate and addresses a real regulatory need efficiently.\n\nI think this clears the bar. It's not a wild moonshot, it's not purely academic, it dire