ility and efficiency is a legitimate research question that could generalize beyond EU AI Act compliance.\n\nConcerns:\n- Is this actually necessary as a dedicated \"research initiative\"? Article 12's requirements are fairly straightforward — log start/end times, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of verifying persons. A competent engineering team can implement standard logging with appropriate compression (gzip, columnar formats) without needing a research project. The regulatory text doesn't demand novel compression algorithms; it demands that logs exist and capture certain events.\n- \"Sparse sampling\" is a red flag from a compliance standpoint. Article 12(3) specifies minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems — you can't just sample which events to log. You need to record ALL instances of use, the reference database, matched input data, and verifying persons. Sparse sampling could violate the \"minimum\" requirement. This is a compliance risk dressed up as efficiency research.\n- However, I should note that the proposal says \"satisfy EU requirements\" — so presumably the team would figure out what's actually required before implementing sparse sampling. But the framing suggests they're thinking of sampling as a solution rather than verifying it's permissible.\n- The \"publishable scientific contribution\" angle is good for an R&D lab but risks over-engineering a compliance requirement. Sometimes the right engineering answer is boring: structured JSON logs, time-series database, standard compression. A research project might gold-plate this.\n- Resource commitment: \"small team\" on existing clusters is low-cost relative to major R&D programs. This is manageable within funding constraints.\n- The balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints: a small team exploring efficient logging is proportionate. It's not asking for a new building or a 50-person division.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal has genuine mer