ch project on \"new logging primitives\" and \"latent-space event mapping systems\" is open-ended, high-risk, with no guaranteed deliverable that maps cleanly to Article 12 compliance.\n- The scope is very broad: \"design new logging primitives AND a latent-space event mapping system to record meaningful state transitions in neural networks.\" This sounds like it could consume significant research resources without a clear endpoint or deliverable timeline.\n- From a funding-constraint perspective, this is expensive R&D with uncertain payoff for compliance purposes. The lab likely already has infrastructure for basic logging (timestamps, input/output records) that satisfies Article 12(3) minimums. The \"semantic\" layer on top is an enhancement, not a requirement per se — the regulation says logs must enable recording of certain events, not that they must be \"intelligent\" or capture \"causal states.\"\n- The phrase \"advances the field of AI interpretability\" signals this is a scientific ambition project. While valuable, from my vantage point I need to ask: can we achieve Article 12 compliance with our existing infrastructure and more modest extensions (structured event schemas, risk-state flagging) rather than inventing entirely new logging primitives?\n- The \"latent-space event mapping system\" for neural networks is particularly ambitious. This implies building interpretability tooling that captures what's happening inside the model — a research frontier problem with no guaranteed solution timeline.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my Lab Director perspective focused on resource efficiency and balancing ambition with constraints: This proposal conflates a compliance requirement (which can be met with well-structured event logging) with a much larger scientific research program (latent-space interpretability, new logging primitives). The regulatory floor in Article 12(3) is specific and achievable: record start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, nat