tion tests, integration tests that trigger a \"use\" and verify all four data points appear in logs. This is very doable with standard testing frameworks.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Adding another gate to CI/CD could slow down prototyping cycles. However, the proposal specifically targets \"external testing environments\" - not internal dev/sandbox. So developers can still iterate freely internally; they just need the logging infrastructure in place before pushing outward. This is a reasonable boundary.\n- The key risk: if the logging tests are brittle or require significant setup (e.g., mocking the reference database, simulating natural person identification), it could add friction. But these are all standard integration test patterns.\n- Actually, from a prototyping standpoint, this is quite good advice. If you're building a prototype that will eventually need to meet Article 12(3) requirements, it's far cheaper and faster to build the logging infrastructure in from day one rather than retrofitting later. A CI gate enforces this discipline without being overly burdensome.\n- The \"cannot be pushed to external testing environments\" constraint is reasonable - internal prototyping should remain fast, but the moment you're about to share with stakeholders or regulators, the logging must work.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal narrows scope to Article 12(3) specifically (Annex III point 1(a) systems). If our lab works on other high-risk categories, this gate wouldn't cover those. But that's a scoping choice, not a flaw in the approach for what it targets.\n- \"Reduces legal risk during prototyping\" - this is slightly overstated. The Act applies to high-risk AI systems when they're placed on the market or put into service. Prototypes strictly internal are likely not yet subject to full compliance obligations. But building compliant infrastructure early is still good engineering practice and reduces later rework.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a technically sound, well-scoped proposal that aligns with standard DevOps practices. The automated testing of logging capabilities is very feasible - these are discrete, verifiable data points. The CI/CD integration point (before external deployment) balances compliance needs against prototyping speed. From my vantage point, this is a high-value, low-complexity intervention that would genuinely improve our engineering output quality and reduce rework risk.\n\nI'd rate this quite highly from a technical viability and prototyping standpoint. The implementation is straightforward, the benefit is clear (early compliance = less rework), and it doesn't unduly slow internal iteration. I'd say around 82-85. It's not a 90+ because: (1) it only covers Article 12(3) specific to one category of high-risk systems, (2) the open-ended requirements in Articles 12(1)-(2) are harder to test automatically and aren't addressed here, (3) there's some risk of brittleness in the tests if logging schemas evolve. But it's a clearly viable, valuable engineering intervention.\n\nI'll approve with a score around 84.\n",
