roject can be significant.\n- Article 12(3) requires specific data points for Annex III point 1(a) systems: period of each use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification. A generic \"standardized API hooks\" approach may not naturally capture domain-specific events like \"the search led to a match\" or \"reference database X was used.\" The middleware would need configurable schemas per project type, which complicates the \"plug-and-play\" claim.\n- Over-engineering risk: If we have only 2-3 high-risk projects, building a centralized framework first is a significant upfront investment before any single project can prototype. For speed of prototyping, this could actually *slow down* early work because teams must wait for the framework to be built and then adapt their code to it.\n- The proposal conflates \"model training pipelines\" with runtime logging. Article 12(1) says logs over the lifetime of the system — this includes deployment/inference time, not just training. Training pipeline integration captures some of this (recording model versions, data used), but the critical operational logs (each use session, input data, matches) happen at inference/deployment time. The proposal's focus on \"model training pipelines\" is somewhat misaligned with what Article 12 actually requires for operational logging.\n- However, it does mention \"automatic recording of events over the lifetime,\" so maybe they mean broader integration. But the specific callout to \"existing model training pipelines\" is a bit narrow.\n\nFrom my vantage point:\n- Technical viability: The concept is viable but the execution details matter enormously. A shared logging library is a well-understood pattern. The risk is in the integration complexity and ensuring it captures all Article 12(3) data points without becoming a rigid constraint that slows prototyping.\n- Speed/quality of prototyping: This could help IF the framework is built quickly and is