        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2723 is about a \"Forensic Traceability Framework for AI Interpretability\" that would:\n1. Develop logs beyond simple timestamps to provide causal explanations for system decisions\n2. Turn mandatory auditing into a mechanism for scientific discovery\n3. Engineer a specialized logging layer capturing latent space transitions and neuron activation patterns\n4. Combine this with required Article 12 metadata\n5. Frame it as transforming regulatory burden into AI interpretability research\n\nLet me assess from my technical viability and prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n- Capturing \"latent space transitions and neuron activation patterns\" at logging granularity is extraordinarily expensive computationally. For large models, recording neuron activations for every inference would be massive in storage (potentially terabytes per day for production systems) and add significant latency to inference pipelines.\n- \"Causal explanations for system decisions\" is an extremely open research problem. We don't have robust methods for causal attribution in deep neural networks yet. This isn't something you can just \"engineer a specialized logging layer\" for - the interpretability science isn't there.\n- The proposal conflates two very different things: (a) meeting Article 12 compliance requirements (which are well-defined and achievable), and (b) solving open research problems in AI interpretability/causal attribution. Mixing these creates enormous scope risk.\n- \"Latent space transitions\" - for which model architecture? This changes fundamentally between transformer, CNN, RNN, etc. A one-size-fits-all logging layer that captures this across different architectures is a massive engineering undertaking.\n- The coupling of interpretability research with compliance