totyping target, and conflates a solvable compliance problem with an open research challenge. From my vantage point of \"can we build this quickly and does it work technically,\" the answer is problematic. The technical feasibility of full causal explanation logging across model architectures is not established, and the storage/latency costs would be prohibitive without significant engineering innovation that hasn't been scoped here.\n\nI'll reject this because:\n1. The core value proposition (causal explanations via latent space/neuron activation capture) relies on unsolved research problems\n2. It cannot be prototyped at the speed and quality I need for compliance delivery\n3. The scope is so broad it lacks a clear engineering path to a working artifact\n4. The Article 12 minimum requirements are achievable without any of this complexity\n\nPromise score: I'd give it maybe 25-30. There's some kernel of value (capturing richer metadata for post-market monitoring), but the specific framing around causal explanations and latent space transitions is too research-heavy to be a near-term prototyping commitment. It's not a bad idea in principle, just not technically viable as a near-term engineering deliverable from my vantage point.\n",
