rn in enterprise software.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- This is where it gets interesting. From my perspective, mandating that ALL high-risk AI prototypes integrate this library from day one could slow down early-stage prototyping. In the exploration phase, you might be iterating rapidly on model architecture, data pipelines, etc., and adding a logging framework adds integration overhead before the solution is even proven viable.\n- However, the proposal argues it \"prevents costly retrofitting.\" There's a trade-off: build-in compliance from the start vs. add later. From a prototyping speed perspective, having a well-designed, minimal-footprint SDK that can be dropped in with minimal code changes would mitigate this concern. If the API is thin and non-intrusive (e.g., decorators, middleware), the overhead is small.\n- The risk is scope creep: \"captures system states and event triggers in real time\" could become a bloated framework that slows down iteration if it's too heavyweight or requires extensive configuration per project.\n- On the positive side, having a common library means engineers don't have to reinvent logging for each new prototype — once you've integrated it once, subsequent projects are faster. This actually speeds up prototyping over time (amortized cost).\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe proposal is technically viable — building an internal logging/telemetry SDK is well within standard engineering capability. The main concern from my vantage point is the risk that a \"unified framework\" becomes bureaucratic overhead that slows down early-stage experimentation, or that the scope (\"system states and event triggers in real time\") is too broad and leads to an unwieldy library. However, if scoped properly as a thin, composable SDK with clear integration patterns per architecture type, it's a solid engineering investment that pays off by preventing rework and ensuring consistent logging quality across prototypes.\n\nI think this clears the