        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P2794 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain concerns: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n**Proposal P2794: High-Risk Classification Audit Process**\n- A formal internal review gateway to determine if a research project falls under high-risk categories\n- Quarterly review board with legal and technical leads\n- Categorize active R&D streams based on intended purpose\n- Prevents waste by ensuring logging infrastructure is only deployed where legally mandatory\n\n**From my Compliance Officer vantage point:**\n\nStrengths:\n1. This is fundamentally a compliance enabler. Article 12 applies to \"high-risk AI systems\" — but the Act's scope depends on classification under Annex III and Annex I. If we can't reliably determine which projects are high-risk, we either (a) over-apply logging infrastructure (waste of resources) or (b) miss obligations (non-compliance, regulatory risk). This is a critical gap-closer.\n2. The quarterly cadence provides periodic reassessment as projects evolve — a research project's intended purpose can shift during development, and if it crosses into high-risk territory mid-stream, we need to catch that.\n3. Legal + technical composition addresses the dual nature of classification: legal interpretation of \"intended purpose\" and Annex III categories requires legal expertise; understanding what the system technically does requires technical expertise.\n4. From an institutional survival standpoint, misclassification (either direction) is a major risk. Over-classification wastes resources but doesn't create regulatory exposure. Under-classification means we fail to implement Article 12 logging for systems that require it — direct non-compliance, potential fines up to 7% of global turnover or €35M (whichever higher), and reputational damage.\n5. Market reputation: demonstrating a formal classification process shows regulators and market partners that we take compliance seriously. It's a governance artifact that supports our compliance narrative.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n1. Quarterly cadence might be too slow for fast-moving R&D. A project could pivot into high-risk territory between quarters, leaving a gap where logging isn't in place when it should be. The Act doesn't give us a \"we didn't know yet\" defense if the intended purpose was arguably high-risk from an earlier point.\n2. \"Intended purpose\" is the key determinant for classification, but in R&D contexts, the intended purpose may not be fully crystallized early on. A research project exploring general-purpose AI might later be deployed in a way that makes it high-risk (e.g., used for biometric categorization). The quarterly board needs to account for foreseeable uses, not just current plans.\n3. This is a process/governance proposal, not an implementation of Article 12 itself. It doesn't build the logging infrastructure — it determines WHERE to deploy it. So it's a necessary precursor but insufficient on its own. However, the proposal correctly positions itself as a gateway/enabler rather than claiming to satisfy Article 12.\n4. Resource commitment: A quarterly board with legal and technical leads requires recurring senior-level attention. In an R&D lab, pulling these people in quarterly has opportunity cost. But compared to the cost of non-compliance or misallocation, this is modest.\n\n**Assessment from compliance vantage point:**\nThis is a strong enabler for regulatory compliance. Without reliable classification, we cannot know where Article 12 obligations attach. This proposal directly addresses the \"which systems are in scope\" question that precedes any implementation of logging requirements. It supports institutional survival by reducing the risk of non-compliance (fines, enforcement action) and protects market reputation through demonstrable governance. The quarterly cadence is a reasonable minimum; I'd want to flag that ad-hoc triggers should also exist for mid-quarter pivots, but as written, it's sound.\n\nThe proposal doesn't directly implement Article 12 logging — it determines scope. But from my vantage point, getting scope right is the first and most critical compliance step. Misallocating resources (either over or under) to logging infrastructure creates both financial waste and regulatory risk.\n\nI'd approve this. It's a clear compliance enabler with direct relevance to my domain. The promise score: it's valuable but not sufficient on its own (doesn't build the actual logging). Also, quarterly might need supplementation. I'll give it around 72-78. Let me settle on 75 — strong governance enabler, directly relevant to compliance scope determination, but has the cadence limitation and is a precursor rather than an implementation.\n\nLet me write my response now.\n",
