rdination overhead between engineering and compliance teams.\n- This is a relatively bounded implementation scope compared to, say, redesigning our entire logging architecture or building novel safety mechanisms.\n\nCons/Concerns:\n- \"Scrapes git commits, MLflow experiments, and data manifests\" — this suggests it's primarily an aggregation/formatting layer. The real question is whether the underlying systems (git, MLflow) actually capture *all* the information Article 12 requires. For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically, we need start/end times of each use, reference database identification, input data for matches, and natural person verification IDs. Some of these may not be trivially available from just git/MLflow — they might require instrumenting the inference/deployment layer too.\n- However, the proposal says \"at a minimum\" and focuses on the general compliance documentation pipeline. The Annex III 1(a) specifics might need additional instrumentation beyond what's described. But the core architecture (auto-aggregation from existing tools into structured output) is sound.\n- Development cost: building scripts to aggregate metadata across multiple systems and present them in a regulatory-compliant format is non-trivial but bounded. It's not an open-ended R&D project — it has clear success criteria (does the output satisfy Article 12 documentation requirements?).\n- This doesn't compete with scientific ambition projects for funding in a way that would be harmful. In fact, by offloading compliance burden from engineers, it *frees up* engineering capacity for actual research.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a high-value, resource-efficient proposal. It leverages existing infrastructure (git, MLflow), addresses a mandatory regulatory requirement with automation rather than headcount, and frees engineering time for actual R&D work. The scope is well-bounded. The main risk is underestimating the instrumentation needed at the deployment/inference layer for ful