o researchers can focus on their science.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Explicitly wraps \"existing model inference engines\" — this is exactly leveraging what we already have rather than building parallel systems.\n- Standardization across projects means auditing and maintenance is simpler, reducing long-term operational costs.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n- There's a development cost up front (building the library), but given that Article 12(1) makes automatic logging mandatory for all high-risk AI systems, this investment is essentially forced by regulation regardless. The question is whether to do it centrally or distributed — central is clearly more efficient.\n- \"Lightweight wrapper\" suggests low complexity and low ongoing maintenance burden, which is good for resource efficiency.\n- One risk: if the middleware becomes a bottleneck or requires all projects to conform to its interface, there could be some friction. But this is a minor concern compared to the alternative of each team building their own logging.\n- It directly addresses Article 12(3)(a) (start/end times) and partially (b)-(d) for Annex III 1(a) systems. For other high-risk categories, it may need extensions, but the core architecture is sound.\n\n**Decision:** This clearly clears my bar. It's a resource-efficient, one-time investment that leverages existing infrastructure, removes repetitive work from teams, and addresses a mandatory regulatory requirement. From my vantage point of resource-allocation efficiency and effective use of existing infrastructure, this is almost ideal — high leverage, low ongoing cost, uses what we already have.\n\n**Promise score:** This is very aligned with my concerns. I'd give it a high score. It's not a 100 because there will be some integration overhead across heterogeneous projects and the \"lightweight\" descriptor needs to be validated in practice (sometimes lightweight wrappers become fragile). But it's strongly