        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate proposal P1703 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n**P1703: High-Risk Project Classification Audit**\n\nThis proposes conducting a comprehensive review of the R&D portfolio to categorize projects by their risk level under the EU AI Act, determining which systems actually require Article 12 overhead. It involves technical workshops pairing project leads with legal experts to map system capabilities against Annex III criteria, then tagging each project in the resource planner as high or low risk.\n\nFrom a Compliance Officer's perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- This is fundamentally a prerequisite step for compliance. Before you can comply with Article 12 (or any other provision), you must correctly identify which of your systems are \"high-risk\" under the Act. Misclassification is a serious compliance failure.\n- The EU AI Act's obligations apply to \"high-risk AI systems.\" If you fail to properly classify, you either (a) miss obligations on systems that ARE high-risk (non-compliance), or (b) over-apply resources to systems that aren't (waste). Both are problematic from a compliance standpoint.\n- Pairing project leads with legal experts is sound — technical capability assessment against Annex III criteria requires both technical and regulatory expertise.\n- From institutional survival: if the lab fails to identify high-risk systems, it faces enforcement action, fines (up to 7% of global turnover or €35M for prohibited practices; up to 3% or €15M for non-compliance with other obligations), and reputational damage. Proper classification is a foundational compliance activity.\n- From market reputation: demonstrating that you have a rigorous process for classifying your systems shows regulators and customers that the institution takes compliance seriously.\n- This directly supports Article 12 compliance by ensuring the lab applies logging requirements to the right systems.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- The proposal says \"tag each project in our resource planner as high or low risk\" — this binary simplification might be too coarse. The EU AI Act has nuanced categories (prohibited, high-risk under Annex I product safety directives, high-risk under Annex III, limited risk, minimal risk). A binary high/low tag might miss the nuance of, say, a system that is high-risk under one Annex III category but also touches prohibited practices. However, for resource planning purposes relative to Article 12 specifically, this might be sufficient as an initial categorization step.\n- \"Series of technical workshops\" — good, but needs to be ongoing, not one-time, as projects evolve and capabilities change. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention ongoing monitoring or reclassification triggers.\n- However, the proposal is fundamentally about getting the classification right, which is the single most important prerequisite for any Article 12 compliance work.\n\n**Assessment from Compliance Officer vantage point:**\nThis is a foundational and necessary step. Without correct classification, all downstream compliance efforts (including Article 12 logging) are either misdirected or missing entirely. The risk of non-compliance due to misclassification is severe — it's not just an Article 12 issue; it could mean the lab is operating high-risk systems without any of the required safeguards (risk management, data governance, technical documentation, logging, transparency, human oversight, accuracy/robustness/cybersecurity). This directly threatens institutional survival.\n\nThe proposal is well-conceived: pairing technical and legal expertise is exactly what's needed for Annex III mapping. The resource efficiency argument (\"prevents wasting expensive engineering resources\") is secondary but valid — the primary compliance rationale is ensuring you don't MISS obligations on systems that are actually high-risk.\n\nOne minor reservation: the binary \"high or low risk\" tagging could be improved to capture the full spectrum, and there should be a reclassification trigger mechanism. But these are implementation details that don't negate the core value of the proposal.\n\nI would approve this. It's a prerequisite for everything else in the compliance program. From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation), getting classification right is arguably the #1 priority before any other Article 12 work begins.\n\nPromise score: This is very high value from a compliance perspective. I'd put it around 85-90. The reason it's not 100 is the binary simplification and lack of explicit ongoing reclassification mechanism, but these are fixable in implementation. The core idea is essential and well-directed.\n\nLet me settle on 87 — very strong, foundational, with minor structural concerns that don't undermine the approval.\n",
