ate in Article 12(1)\n- Versioned database with unique hashes provides traceability and auditability - regulators can verify claims\n- Reduces manual error risk in record-keeping, which is a significant compliance failure mode\n- Creates an auditable trail that would be essential during regulatory inspections or market surveillance\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" is addressed by the versioned/registry approach\n- The dual benefit (compliance + debugging/reproducibility) makes it more likely to get internal buy-in and sustained maintenance\n\nCons/Risks:\n- Scope limitation: Article 12(3)(b) only applies to Annex III point 1(a) systems. If we develop other high-risk categories, this alone doesn't cover all of Article 12. However, the proposal is scoped appropriately - it's addressing a specific gap.\n- \"Removes the need for manual record-keeping\" - as a compliance officer, I'd note that automation helps but we still need governance over what gets logged and retention periods. The proposal doesn't mention data retention policy aligned with system lifetime requirements.\n- MLflow/DVC are good tools but the proposal should specify who is responsible for maintaining the registry's integrity (access controls, tamper-evidence). A hash alone isn't sufficient if someone can alter the registry itself.\n- Doesn't explicitly address Article 12(3)(d) - identification of natural persons involved in verification. But that's a separate requirement and this proposal doesn't claim to cover it.\n\nOverall: This is a well-targeted, technically sound proposal that directly addresses a specific regulatory obligation (Article 12(3)(b)) using established tools with an automated approach. From a compliance standpoint, having an automated, versioned, hash-verified registry of reference databases is exactly what a regulator would expect to see during a market surveillance check. It reduces our exposure to non-compliance findings on traceability. The dual benefit argument makes it mor