creep territory. Different high-risk AI systems (Annex III categories) have different logging requirements per Article 12(3). A truly unified layer needs to handle these variations.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal says it \"acts as a universal wrapper for model execution\" — this suggests it sits on top of existing pipelines rather than replacing them. That's good for infrastructure reuse.\n- However, it requires all projects to integrate the library, which is an organizational change that may encounter resistance or create integration debt.\n- The \"secure, immutable storage\" requirement implies new infrastructure (or at least configuration of existing infrastructure) — this is a capex/opex consideration.\n\n**Strengths from my vantage point:**\n- Avoids N separate logging implementations across N projects — economies of scale\n- Reduces researcher time spent on compliance plumbing (they're expensive, their time is better on science)\n- Standardized approach means audit readiness is built-in rather than retrofitted per project\n- API-driven design is modular and maintainable\n\n**Weaknesses/risks from my vantage point:**\n- \"Universal wrapper\" for diverse projects is ambitious; different model architectures (LLMs, CV, time-series) have very different execution paths. A truly universal abstraction may not exist without significant complexity.\n- Building vs. buying: could an existing observability/logging platform (e.g., OpenTelemetry-based solutions) be adapted rather than building from scratch? The proposal doesn't address this.\n- Maintenance burden falls on the lab long-term — who maintains this middleware as the AI stack evolves?\n- If the lab's portfolio of high-risk systems is small, ROI may not justify the build cost vs. per-project compliance efforts.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a solid infrastructure investment IF the lab has multiple (say 3+) concurrent high-risk AI projects. It addresses real resource-alloc