ment rather than a massive new build. That's more manageable.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Positive: building on existing systems as plug-ins.\n- Concern: does the lab have researchers with expertise in interpretability/tracing in production AI? If not, this becomes an expensive hiring/external collaboration commitment.\n\n**My decision:**\nThis is a borderline case. The core idea (enhanced traceability beyond minimum compliance) has merit and builds on existing infrastructure through plug-ins. But the ambition level (\"deep traceability,\" \"real-time mapping to precise reference database entries\") risks over-committing resources relative to what's actually required for compliance, especially if our systems aren't all Annex III 1(a). \n\nHowever, the proposal does say \"prototype these as plug-in modules\" — that's a bounded, incremental commitment rather than an open-ended research program. And it addresses a real requirement we must meet anyway. The competitive advantage angle provides additional justification.\n\nI think I'd lean toward approve, but with moderate confidence. It's not a clear-cut reject because:\n- It leverages existing infrastructure (plug-in approach)\n- It addresses a genuine regulatory need\n- It's framed as prototyping, not full-scale deployment\n- The competitive angle adds value beyond compliance\n\nIt's not a 90+ score because:\n- The \"deep traceability\" ambition could balloon in scope and cost\n- Real-time precise mapping is technically very hard for complex systems\n- Funding constraints mean we can't afford open-ended research without clear regulatory payoff milestones\n\nI'll approve with a promise score around 58-65. Let me settle on 60. It's promising but the ambition-to-resource ratio concerns me slightly, and the \"real-time precise mapping\" could be a scope trap. But the plug-in architecture and existing-systems leverage save it from rejection.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My domain is specifically: resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, effective use of existing R&D infrastructure. \n\nThe proposal:\n- Uses existing systems (good for infrastructure utilization) ✓\n- Is a \"research project\" with open-ended goals (\"developing methods\") — this is where it conflicts with funding constraints ✗\n- The competitive advantage framing helps justify the spend beyond minimum compliance ✓\n- Plug-in modularity = efficient resource allocation across multiple systems ✓\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 62. It's solid but the research ambition vs. bounded compliance need creates a tension that keeps me from higher scores.\n",
