        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2705\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12 mandates that high-risk systems technically allow automatic log recording and retain the specified data points; it does not require building a purpose-built web dashboard or front-end application for auditors. Committing engineering resources to design, build, secure, and maintain a new UI layer (scoped API keys, query interface, display of verifying-personnel identities) is a significant cost that diverts staff from hardening the actual compliance infrastructure—ensuring logs are immutable, complete, structured per Annex III 1(a), and retained for the system's lifetime. A read-only scoped API endpoint or controlled structured-export mechanism against the existing immutable log store would satisfy auditor traceability at a fraction of the development effort and with a smaller security surface, aligning far better with our limited implementation budget.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 25\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The proposal P2705 describes building a \"Regulatory Compliance Audit Dashboard\" — a read-only web application that lets external auditors query an immutable log database to view system logs and human verification signatures, with access controlled via scoped API keys.\n\nLet me assess this from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with:\n- Resource-allocation efficiency\n- Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints\n- Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\n\nKey considerations:\n\n1. **Resource allocation**: Building a dedicated web application is a significant engineering investment. The proposal describes building a \"web application that queries our immutable log database to display specific matches and the identities of verifying personnel.\" This is a new software development project on top of what should already exist (the logging system required by Article 12(1)).\n\n2. **Is this actually needed?** Article 12 requires:\n   - Automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime — this is about the logging mechanism itself\n   - Logging capabilities for specific purposes (risk situations, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring)\n   - For Annex III 1(a) systems: specific minimum data points\n\nThe article does NOT mandate a \"dashboard\" or \"web application.\" It mandates that logs exist and are technically capable of being recorded. The technical documentation (Recital 71) should contain information to assess compliance and facilitate post-market monitoring, but it doesn't prescribe a UI for external auditors. Auditors can verify through the technical documentation, direct access to logs, or API queries — a purpose-built web dashboard is not a regulatory requirement.\n\n3. **Funding constraints**: Building a full web application (front-end, back-end, API key management, security hardening) is a non-trivial engineering effort. The lab's resources are better spent on the core R&D and the actual compliance requirements (ensuring logs are properly structured, immutable, retained for the right duration). A dashboard is a nice-to-have convenience layer, not a regulatory obligation.\n\n4. **Effective use of existing infrastructure**: If we already have an immutable log database (which would be part of meeting Article 12(1)), providing read-only access via scoped API keys to auditors is relatively straightforward and doesn't require building a full web application. The \"web application\" framing implies front-end development, UI/UX work, etc., which is over-engineering for an auditor-facing tool. Auditors typically prefer raw data access or structured exports rather than a pretty dashboard.\n\n5. **Security concerns**: Exposing \"identities of verifying personnel\" through a web interface introduces additional attack surface. The proposal acknowledges this by saying it avoids \"exposing sensitive internal development environments,\" but building any new external-facing service increases the security perimeter. From an efficiency standpoint, a scoped API endpoint or a controlled data export mechanism would achieve the same auditability with less resource investment and smaller attack surface.\n\n6. **Does it advance scientific ambition?** No. This is purely an administrative/compliance convenience tool. It doesn't contribute to R&D output, model development, or any scientific objective.\n\nMy assessment: The core need (auditor access to logs) is legitimate but the proposed solution (building a web application/dashboard) is over-engineered and misallocates engineering resources. A simpler mechanism (scoped API read access, structured log exports for audit periods) would satisfy the same compliance need at a fraction of the cost and development time. The proposal as stated commits significant resources to building UI infrastructure that isn't required by the regulation and doesn't leverage what should already be in place (the immutable log database with queryable interfaces).\n\nDecision: Reject. The regulatory requirement is for logs to exist and be technically accessible, not for a purpose-built dashboard application. The resource cost of building and maintaining a web app exceeds what's needed. A scoped API or controlled data access mechanism would suffice at much lower cost.\n\nPromise score: This addresses a real need (auditor access) but in an over-engineered way that misallocates scarce engineering resources away from core compliance infrastructure and R&D. I'd give it maybe 25-30 — the underlying problem is valid, but the solution as proposed is inefficient and not what the regulation requires.\n",
