uiring manual tracking - this addresses the \"kept up to date appropriately throughout the lifetime\" requirement in Recital 71.\n- Risk: LLM-generated documentation may be inaccurate or miss critical nuances, potentially creating compliance liability if regulators find gaps. This could lead to rework costs that exceed the savings.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Strong alignment. It builds on git, code comments, experiment trackers - all already in place. No new hardware, no new data collection systems needed. The pipeline is software-only.\n- However, \"substantial modification\" detection from VCS alone may be insufficient - not all substantial modifications manifest as clear VCS events (e.g., model retraining with same architecture but different hyperparameters might or might not trigger depending on implementation). This could create false confidence about compliance coverage.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-conceived proposal that addresses a genuine operational need with existing infrastructure. The resource efficiency argument is strong - it converts ongoing manual documentation labor into automated synthesis. The main risk is accuracy/reliability of LLM-generated regulatory documents, but as an aid to (not replacement for) human review, this is manageable. From my vantage point on resource allocation and infrastructure utilization, this is a high-value proposal that makes the lab more efficient without requiring major new capital expenditure.\n\nI'd rate this fairly highly - it's practical, leverages existing systems, addresses a real ongoing cost (scientist time spent on compliance), and aligns well with the \"up to date throughout lifetime\" requirement. I'll approve it. Promise score: around 78-82. It's very good but not perfect because of the LLM reliability concerns and the question of whether VCS-based substantial modification detection is robust enough for all our system types.\n",
