t of the compliance picture but not the core automatic event-logging requirement.\n\nWait, let me re-read. The proposal says \"create live documentation\" and \"reduces the manual burden of maintaining up-to-date records.\" Recital 71 does talk about technical documentation containing information on algorithms, data, training, testing, validation processes. So this is addressing the documentation side. But Article 12 itself (the binding text) is primarily about automatic logging capabilities. The proposal seems to address the adjacent compliance need (technical documentation upkeep) rather than the core Article 12(1) requirement of \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\"\n\nActually, re-reading Recital 71: \"This requires keeping records and the availability of technical documentation, containing information which is necessary to assess the compliance...\" So there's both a record-keeping/logging obligation (Article 12) AND a technical documentation requirement. The proposal addresses the latter — automating the maintenance of technical documentation. This is complementary to but not the same as Article 12's core logging requirement.\n\nHowever, from my resource-allocation perspective, this is still valuable:\n- It uses existing infra (MLflow) → low new capital expenditure\n- It automates a recurring labor cost → ongoing savings\n- The scope is bounded and achievable with current engineering capacity\n- It addresses a real compliance gap (documentation drift over system lifetime)\n\nBut the concern about scope mismatch is important. If leadership approves this thinking it \"handles Article 12,\" we'd still need separate work for the actual automatic event-logging infrastructure. So there's a risk of misallocation if this is positioned as THE Article 12 solution rather than A component.\n\nFrom my vantage point, I should judge strictly on its own merits:\n- Does it use existing infrastructure efficiently? Yes (MLflow already exists)\n- Is the resource commitment proportionate? Yes — an integration layer + templates is a moderate engineering project, not a multi-year build\n- Does it address a real efficiency problem? Yes — manual documentation maintenance is a known bottleneck\n- Is the ambition balanced with constraints? Reasonably — it's scoped to what MLflow already captures\n\nThe main risk is scope misunderstanding (it doesn't fully satisfy Article 12 by itself), but as a component of the broader compliance effort, it's a well-targeted use of engineering resources on existing infrastructure.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's efficient, uses what we have, bounded in scope, and addresses a genuine recurring cost. The promise score should reflect that it's solid but not transformative — it's a good operational efficiency play within the compliance context, but it doesn't cover the full Article 12 picture on its own. I'd say around 72-78. It's clearly worth doing from my perspective, but with the caveat that it's one piece of a larger puzzle and the \"templates aligned with Article 12\" part needs careful regulatory input to avoid false confidence.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 75.\n",
