otype doesn't yet have full validation results or defined risk parameters. There needs to be a clear boundary between \"prototype/experiment branch\" and \"production branch.\"\n- The Git-integrated trigger approach is clean because it piggybacks on existing developer workflows - low friction, high adoption potential.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Guarantees we satisfy Recital 71 requirements during sudden regulatory inspections\" is a strong claim. The system helps with versioning and traceability of changes, but the content quality of the documentation still depends on what humans write into the metadata fields. It ensures the *structure* is maintained but not necessarily the *substance*.\n- The \"automatic sync\" of training data versions to a technical file is technically challenging at scale - you need a model registry (MLflow, Weights & Biases) and a data versioning system, plus mapping between them. This isn't trivial to prototype quickly.\n- However, the core mechanism (Git hooks + CI gates for metadata) is very prototypable in weeks, not months.\n\n**From my vantage point:**\nThe proposal has a technically sound core (CI/CD integration for compliance metadata enforcement) that maps well to existing engineering practices. The \"automatic sync\" of all data types is ambitious but buildable incrementally. The Git-integrated trigger approach is pragmatic and doesn't require reinventing developer workflows. It addresses the Record-keeping article's requirement for traceability in a way that fits naturally into how we already ship code.\n\nHowever, I'd note that:\n1. The scope as stated is large - it touches data engineering, ML pipeline infrastructure, AND compliance tooling simultaneously.\n2. \"Guarantees\" satisfaction of Recital 71 is overclaiming - it's a necessary enabler but not sufficient alone.\n3. For prototyping speed, the CI gate mechanism is very fast to implement (days), but the full \"living repository\" with auto-synced training data versions and validation results could take months to get right.\n\nOverall: This is technically viable at its core, aligns well with how we already work (Git-based workflows, CI/CD), and if scoped incrementally, could be prototyped quickly for the enforcement mechanism while building out the sync infrastructure over time. It's a strong proposal from an engineering feasibility standpoint. I'd approve it but note that the \"full\" version is a multi-quarter project while the core CI gate is weeks.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. The technical approach is sound and fits existing workflows, but the full scope as described (automatic syncing of all data types) has non-trivial complexity that could slow prototyping if not phased well.\n",
