        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\n\nMy domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P1036 is about \"Privacy-Preserving Verifiable Logging Protocols\" — a research project to develop cryptographic methods (zero-knowledge proofs, secure multi-party computation) for recording event logs that allow regulatory auditing without exposing sensitive training data.\n\nLet me assess this strictly against Article 12:\n\nArticle 12 requires:\n1. High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\n2. Logging capabilities must enable recording of events relevant to: (a) identifying risk situations, (b) facilitating post-market monitoring, (c) monitoring operation.\n3. For biometric categorization (Annex III 1(a)), specific minimum logging requirements include start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, and identification of natural persons involved in verification.\n\nNow let me evaluate P1036 from a compliance perspective:\n\n**Rationale:**\n\nThe proposal is framed as a \"research project\" to explore cryptographic methods. Let me think about what this means for compliance:\n\n1. **Article 12 requires actual logging capabilities.** The regulation says systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" This is a mandatory technical requirement, not an aspirational research goal. Article 12(3) specifically requires recording of input data that led to matches — this seems to directly conflict with a ZKP approach where raw inputs are hidden from the auditor. The regulation requires the *system* to record these things; it doesn't say they must be presented in plaintext, but the requirement is about the system's capability to produce logs containing specific information.\n\