ctually built compliant logging systems. The regulatory timeline for high-risk AI system provisions (August 2026) is approaching.\n\n6. **The proposal is too abstract and exploratory.** \"We will explore\" is not an implementation commitment. As compliance officer, I need concrete, deliverable solutions that meet specific regulatory text within defined timelines. A ZKP research project might take years before it's even applicable, let alone integrated into our AI systems' logging infrastructure.\n\n7. **Risk of non-compliance:** If we pursue this as our primary approach to Article 12 and the regulators interpret the requirement as demanding accessible logs (not just cryptographically verifiable hashes), we'd be non-compliant. The regulation says \"automatic recording of events\" — it doesn't specify the format must be plaintext, but the specific items in 12(3) like \"identification of natural persons\" suggest actual identifiable data needs to be recorded and retrievable.\n\nMy assessment: This is a scientifically interesting research direction that addresses a real tension (data protection vs. auditability), but from a compliance standpoint, it's too exploratory, potentially misaligned with the specific requirements of Article 12(3), and doesn't deliver a near-term compliance solution. It could even create risk if leadership mistakes progress on this for progress toward Article 12 compliance.\n\nI would reject this. It's not an implementation proposal; it's a research exploration that may or may not align with what the regulation actually requires. The specific requirements in 12(3) seem to demand actual data recording, not cryptographic proofs of data existence. And the timeline doesn't support exploratory research when we have binding obligations coming.\n\nPromise score: I'd give it maybe 20-25. It identifies a real tension and the cryptographic approaches are interesting, but from my vantage point as compliance officer focused on institutional survival and meet