igh-risk AI experiments\" shows breadth\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns from a Compliance perspective:\n- The proposal focuses heavily on \"training metadata\" and \"scientific reproducibility.\" Article 12 is about the operational lifetime of deployed high-risk systems, not just training provenance. Recital 71 does mention training data documentation, but Article 12's operative requirements (especially paragraph 3) are about runtime logging of specific events during use.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address the specific Article 12(3) requirements: reference database used for checks, input data that led to a match, identification of natural persons in verification. These are critical and non-negotiable compliance elements.\n- \"Effortless regulatory compliance\" is an overstatement - compliance requires mapping specific log fields to specific regulatory requirements. A generic middleware might miss the exact granularity needed.\n- The proposal emphasizes \"scientific reproducibility\" as a co-benefit, which is nice but not the primary compliance concern. Compliance is about demonstrating to regulators that specific events are captured, not about reproducing experiments.\n- It says \"across all high-risk AI experiments\" - this is good scope coverage, but it's framed around \"experiments\" rather than deployed systems in their operational lifetime. Article 12(1) says \"over the lifetime of the system,\" which includes post-deployment operation, not just R&D phase.\n- The proposal doesn't address the specific event categories in Article 12(2)(a), (b), (c) - risk identification events, post-market monitoring facilitation, and operational monitoring per Article 26(5).\n\nHowever, as a compliance officer:\n- The core architectural approach (unified logging middleware/API) is sound and necessary\n- It does address automatic recording (the fundamental requirement of Art. 12(1))\n- It covers runtime events which maps to the operational monitoring needs\n- Data versioning and timestamps are relevant\n\nMy assessment: This is a reasonable foundational proposal that addresses part of Article 12's requirements but has notable gaps. It's heavily weighted toward training provenance and scientific reproducibility rather than the specific operational logging requirements that regulators will audit. The \"effortless compliance\" claim is problematic from a compliance perspective - it suggests the framework might not be carefully mapped to each specific regulatory requirement. However, as a starting infrastructure investment, it does move in the right direction.\n\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):\n- This is worth pursuing because without any automatic logging infrastructure, we cannot comply with Article 12 at all\n- But I'd need to ensure it specifically addresses Art. 12(3) requirements and the lifetime scope beyond just experiments\n- The \"effortless\" framing concerns me - compliance officers know nothing about regulatory compliance is effortless\n- Still, the core investment direction is correct\n\nI'll approve this but with a moderate promise score because while the direction is right, the proposal as stated has compliance gaps that would need to be addressed in implementation. It's not a complete compliance solution but it's a necessary foundation. I'd say around 55-65 range. Let me settle on 60 - it's a solid foundation but the specific regulatory mapping and lifetime scope concerns keep it from being higher, while the fundamental direction (automatic logging infrastructure) keeps it well above rejection territory.\n",
