s builds trust with clients and partners.\n\nCons/Concerns:\n- The proposal says \"develop algorithmic patterns to scan Article 12 logs\" — this is a significant R&D effort. The compliance obligation under Art. 12 is that the system *shall technically allow for automatic recording*. It doesn't mandate an automated forensic analysis layer on top of those logs. However, having one would be a strong demonstration of compliance with the broader framework (Art. 72, Art. 79).\n- The proposal frames it as converting \"mandatory record-keeping into an intellectual asset\" — this is more of an R&D/engineering framing. From a pure compliance perspective, what matters is that we can demonstrate traceability and respond to incidents within required timeframes (e.g., Article 73 serious incident reporting).\n- Risk: if the forensic tool itself has bugs or misses events, it could create a false sense of security. We'd still need human oversight. But this is an implementation concern, not a reason to reject the proposal.\n- The scope seems well-aligned with what compliance actually requires: detecting risk-triggering events, identifying substantial modifications, and supporting post-market monitoring.\n\nFrom my specific vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):\n- This directly supports multiple Article 12(2) sub-points.\n- It strengthens our ability to respond to regulator inquiries and audits.\n- It reduces the risk of missing a reportable incident (which could lead to penalties under the Act).\n- It enhances market reputation by demonstrating sophisticated compliance infrastructure.\n- It's not just \"nice to have\" — it operationalizes what the Act effectively requires us to be able to do (identify risk events, monitor post-market, detect substantial modifications).\n\nThis is a strong proposal from a compliance perspective. It transforms passive log storage into active compliance capability. I would approve this with high confidence.\n\