g patterns and not need to solve the same problem 50 times across projects.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Building an SDK is a significant engineering undertaking. Who builds it? Takes engineers away from research.\n- Maintenance burden: as new models, architectures, and deployment patterns emerge, the SDK needs updating. This is ongoing cost.\n- \"Every prototype is compliant by default\" - this could slow down prototyping if the overhead of the logging layer is significant. But timestamps and input hashes are lightweight operations.\n- The proposal addresses Article 12(1) well (automatic recording) and partially 12(2) (traceability). It's less clear how it handles 12(3) specific requirements for biometric/identity systems, but those may be out of scope for a general SDK.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is actually quite good. The unified approach prevents duplicated effort across projects. A wrapper around existing execution environments is low-friction. The key question is whether the upfront build cost and ongoing maintenance are justified versus each project handling it individually. For a lab with multiple high-risk AI projects, the answer is almost certainly yes - the amortized cost per project drops significantly.\n\nThe risk is that building an SDK diverts engineering resources from actual research. But if we don't do this centrally, every project team will spend time on it anyway, and they'll do it inconsistently (waste). The centralized approach also ensures quality and consistency of compliance.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a pragmatic, efficient use of resources that directly addresses a regulatory requirement in a way that scales across the lab. It uses existing infrastructure (model execution environment) rather than requiring new platforms. The \"compliant by default\" design reduces ongoing per-project effort.\n\nHowever, I should note some limitations:\n- It doesn't fully address 12(3) for biomet