        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2919\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal vastly over-scopes Article 12's requirement (event-level logging: timestamps, reference databases, input matches, verifier IDs) by mandating capture of internal state activations, weight shifts, and full decision paths across every high-risk project, then funnels it into a purpose-built centralized research lake. From a resource-allocation standpoint, this commits disproportionate engineering and data-infrastructure spend to speculative secondary research value rather than the targeted compliance obligation, and the 'standardized telemetry API across all projects' creates ongoing maintenance drag that will compete directly with funded R&D programmes; the scientific-ambition justification ('goldmine of empirical data') is not a budget line and does not offset the implementation cost.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 38\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director focused on resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal P2919 is: \"Automated Provenance Framework for Model Interpretability\" - a comprehensive logging layer capturing internal state activations, weight shifts, and decision paths alongside mandatory event markers. It proposes developing and integrating a standardized telemetry API across all high-risk projects to feed rich data into a centralized research lake for retrospective analysis, positioning it as transforming compliance into empirical data for studying AI failure modes.\n\nLet me assess this from my resource-allocation efficiency perspective:\n\n**What Article 12 actually requires:**\n- Automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime\n- Events relevant to identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically: start/end times, reference database, input data matches, identification of natural persons in verification\n\nThe proposal goes WELL beyond what's required. It captures \"internal state activations, weight shifts, and decision paths\" — these are not mandated by Article 12. The article asks for event-level logging (what happened), not internal model state logging (how the model internally computed). \n\n**Resource considerations:**\n- Building a \"comprehensive logging layer\" capturing internal state activations and weight shifts is a massive engineering undertaking. This requires instrumenting every model at inference time to capture activation tensors, tracking weight changes, and recording full decision paths.\n- A \"standardized telemetry API across all high-risk projects\" means touching every project's architecture — significant integration overhead.\n- A \"centralized research lake for retrospective analysis\" means building data infrastructure (storage, processing pipelines, access controls) that goes well beyond compliance needs.\n- The data volume from capturing internal state activations at inference time would be enormous — potentially orders of magnitude larger than what Article 12 requires. Storage and compute costs would be substantial.\n\n**Scientific ambition vs. funding constraints:**\n- The proposal explicitly positions the excess as \"goldmine of empirical data for studying AI failure modes.\" This is a classic scope-creep justification: doing far more than required, justified by potential secondary research benefits.\n- From my vantage point, this conflates a compliance obligation with a research program. The lab's R&D infrastructure should be allocated to its core mission. Building a telemetry and data-lake infrastructure is an IT/data-engineering project, not fundamentally an AI R&D project. It consumes engineering resources that could go toward actual model development or validation work.\n- \"Retrospective analysis\" of failure modes is valuable research, but it's speculative — the proposal assumes the data will be useful without demonstrating a clear research plan that justifies the infrastructure investment.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Does the lab already have logging/telemetry infrastructure? If so, can Article 12 compliance be met by extending what exists rather than building new \"comprehensive\" systems?\n- The proposal seems to assume a greenfield build (\"We will develop and integrate\") rather than assessing whether existing monitoring tools suffice for the regulatory minimum.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core requirement of Article 12 is achievable with targeted event-level logging — relatively straightforward engineering. This proposal builds an elaborate internal-state capture system justified primarily by speculative research value. From a resource-allocation standpoint, this is expensive over-engineering relative to the actual compliance obligation. The \"research lake\" and \"standardized telemetry API across all projects\" represent significant ongoing maintenance costs that will compete with funded R&D programs.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: if some of this infrastructure already exists or is cheaply extensible, and if there's a clear research roadmap tied to it, the value proposition improves. But as stated, the proposal is ambitious in scope relative to what's needed, and the justification for the excess (\"goldmine\") is hand-wavy from a budget-justification perspective.\n\nI think this falls below my bar for approval. The compliance portion could be handled more efficiently. The research-lake ambition, while scientifically interesting, doesn't justify the resource commitment at this stage without a funded research program explicitly requesting it. I'll reject it but give it moderate promise because the underlying idea of leveraging compliance data has some merit — just not at this scope or without clearer scoping.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 35-40. The core idea has value (using compliance logging to inform research), but the proposed implementation is over-scoped for a compliance response and would consume disproportionate resources. It's not a terrible idea, but it's not one I'd commit scarce implementation resources to without much more scoping and a separate research budget justification.\n",
