oject with its own maintenance burden.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: This is where I need to think carefully. Does the lab already have monitoring infrastructure? Most AI labs that run production systems have some form of model monitoring, drift detection, or performance dashboards. If so, this proposal might be repurposing and extending existing tooling rather than building from scratch. The \"baseline of expected performance\" concept suggests we'd need to establish reference distributions for each system's outputs — which could be data-intensive but is a one-time setup per model.\n\nHowever, there are concerns:\n- \"Proactively identifies regulatory triggers\" — this is somewhat vague. What exactly constitutes a \"regulatory trigger\"? The regulation requires logs that ENABLE identification of risks, not an automated alerting system that does it for you. There's a difference between having the capability (Article 12 requirement) and building a full surveillance product.\n- \"Protecting the institution from negligence\" — this is a legal/insurance framing, not really an R&D efficiency argument. It's defensive spending.\n- The scope could balloon: \"anomaly detection tool that scans live operational logs against a baseline of expected performance\" — how many models? What granularity? How often do baselines need updating as models are retrained? This could become a significant ongoing maintenance burden.\n\nFrom my resource-allocation perspective:\n- If we're already building logging per Article 12(1), adding basic threshold alerts is low marginal cost and high compliance value. I'd lean toward that being part of the logging implementation, not a separate project.\n- A full \"anomaly detection tool\" with baselines is more ambitious and could consume significant engineering resources for ongoing maintenance (baselines drift, new models need new baselines, false positive tuning, etc.).\n- The proposal as framed seems to conflate compliance infrastructure (required) with a broader monitoring product (optional but potentially valuable).\n\nThe key question: Is this efficiently using existing infrastructure, or does it create a parallel system? If the lab already has MLOps monitoring, this should be integrated, not built separately. If not, then we're building two things at once (logging + anomaly detection) which is reasonable as long as they share architecture.\n\nI think this is moderately promising but has efficiency concerns:\n- The core insight (analyze logs for risk signals) aligns with Article 12(2)(a) and is essentially required\n- But the framing as a separate \"system\" risks duplication or scope creep\n- The anomaly detection component could be expensive to maintain over time relative to its compliance value\n- It's not leveraging existing infrastructure in an obvious way unless we specify integration with current MLOps tooling\n\nI'll lean toward approve but at a moderate promise level. The core function is aligned with what Article 12 effectively requires, and if built efficiently on top of the logging infrastructure we must already create, it represents good resource allocation. But I'm concerned about scope creep and ongoing maintenance costs for a full anomaly detection system that goes beyond minimum compliance.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My bar is: does this clear the threshold for committing scarce implementation resources? \n\nThe argument FOR: Article 12(2)(a) says logs must enable \"identifying situations that may result in risk.\" Having logs but no mechanism to actually scan them means we're technically compliant with 12(1) but failing at the purpose stated in Recital 71. Building a scanning layer is arguably part of making the logging system functional for its intended purpose.\n\nThe argument AGAINST: The regulation doesn't mandate an automated anomaly detection tool. It mandates that logs EXIST and ENABLE identification. A human reviewing logs periodically could satisfy this. An automated system is more efficient but also more expensive to build and maintain. Is the marginal compliance value of automation over manual review worth the engineering cost?\n\nGiven my focus on resource efficiency: if we can integrate basic threshold monitoring into our existing logging stack at low marginal cost, approve. If it requires building a separate \"anomaly detection tool\" with its own pipeline, that's a bigger commitment. The proposal as written says \"implement an anomaly detection tool\" which sounds like a new build rather than extending existing infrastructure.\n\nI'll approve at a moderate level — the direction is right and aligns with regulatory purpose, but I want to flag that it should be implemented as an extension of the Article 12 logging system rather than a parallel project, and scoped tightly to minimum viable alerting rather than a full surveillance product.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 55-65. It's aligned directionally, uses infrastructure we must build anyway (logging), but has scope risks and ongoing maintenance costs that concern me from an efficiency standpoint. Let me settle on 58.\n",
