ems (or will develop more), the amortized cost per system decreases significantly compared to building logging per project.\n- The proposal says \"every model deployment\" — this could be over-engineering for lower-complexity systems where lighter-weight logging might suffice under Article 12's proportionality language (\"appropriate to the intended purpose\").\n- Maintenance burden: who owns and maintains this library long-term? If it becomes a single point of failure or a bottleneck, that's a resource concern.\n- The proposal doesn't mention cost estimates, timeline, or how it fits within existing infrastructure budgets. But as a screening exercise, I'm evaluating the idea itself, not whether the budget is already allocated.\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency assessment:**\nThis is fundamentally an efficiency play — one build serves many deployments. It converts per-project compliance costs into a one-time platform investment plus marginal integration costs. For a lab with multiple high-risk systems in pipeline, this is clearly more resource-efficient than ad-hoc solutions. The CI/CD mandate ensures it's not optional, reducing the coordination overhead of chasing teams for compliance.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\nThis doesn't really constrain scientific ambition — it's an infrastructure layer that sits alongside research work. It does require engineering resources that could otherwise go to model development, but the alternative (non-compliance risk, or per-project logging builds) is more expensive overall. It's a reasonable trade-off.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\nCI/CD integration leverages existing deployment pipelines rather than building parallel systems. The \"standardized software library\" approach suggests reuse of internal tooling patterns. This aligns with using what we already have.\n\nOverall, from my vantage point, this is a strong resource-efficiency proposal. It's the kind of infrastructure investment that pays for itself across multiple projects and reduces long-term compliance risk. The main reservation is whether \"every model deployment\" is proportionate — but as a framework design principle (with configurable granularity), it's sound.\n\nI'd rate this fairly high from my perspective. It's not 100 because there are some scoping questions (does it cover Article 12(3) specifics for Annex III systems? What about the \"reference database\" and \"input data match\" logging which might require domain-specific implementation beyond a generic library?). But it's a clearly efficient use of resources.\n\nI'll approve with a promise score around 78-82. Let me settle on 80.\n",
