t could balloon.\n- The proposal mentions \"the engineering team will develop\" — this commits lab resources upfront. If the scope creeps or the architecture assumptions are wrong, you've sunk significant cost into infrastructure that doesn't fit.\n- It says \"timestamps, input data, and user IDs consistently\" — but Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems requires more specific things (reference database, input data leading to a match, identification of natural persons in verification). The proposal's description is somewhat generic and may not capture all the domain-specific logging needs without additional per-project configuration.\n- There's a risk that \"unified\" becomes a bottleneck: if one team has a novel architecture that doesn't fit the hooks, they either wait for middleware updates (delaying science) or build their own thing anyway (defeating the purpose).\n\n**My assessment from the resource-allocation efficiency vantage point:**\nThis is fundamentally a good idea. The core insight — build compliance infrastructure once, reuse it across projects — is exactly what an R&D institution with limited funding should do. It prevents fragmentation of effort and ensures that the mandatory logging (which Article 12 makes non-negotiable for high-risk systems) doesn't eat into each team's research budget repeatedly.\n\nHowever, the proposal as stated is somewhat thin on implementation detail. \"Universal API hooks\" across \"diverse AI architectures\" could be an overstatement of what's achievable in a single engineering sprint. The risk is that the initial development cost is underestimated and the middleware becomes too rigid or too generic to actually save time downstream.\n\nStill, from my vantage point, the resource-allocation logic is sound: centralized compliance infrastructure is cheaper than distributed reinvention. The effective use of existing R&D infrastructure angle also supports this — you're creating a shared asset that compounds in value as more projects adopt it.\n\nI'd approve this with moderate confidence. It's directionally correct and addresses a real efficiency concern. My promise score should reflect that I'd back it but want to see scoping work before full commitment. Maybe around 72-78? It's clearly valuable, but the \"universal\" claim makes me slightly cautious about whether the engineering investment will actually pay off as described without iteration.\n\nLet me settle on:\n- Rationale: The core resource-efficiency argument is strong — centralized compliance infrastructure prevents N teams from duplicating logging work, freeing research funds for scientific ambition. However, the claim of \"universal\" hooks across diverse architectures risks underestimating engineering scope and creating a rigid bottleneck if novel systems don't fit cleanly.\n- Decision: approve\n- Promise: 74\n\nThis feels right. I'd back it because the efficiency logic is sound and the regulatory floor (Article 12) makes this spending non-discretionary anyway — better to do it once centrally than N times redundantly. But I'm not at 90+ because the \"universal\" framing could mask significant per-architecture adaptation work that would erode the savings.\n",
