erent research teams\" — addresses the institutional challenge of multiple teams; ensures uniform compliance posture.\n- \"Minimizes manual reporting burden on researchers while guaranteeing absolute regulatory traceability\" — operational sustainability of compliance (if researchers manually log things, it's error-prone and likely non-compliant over time).\n\n**Potential gaps or concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The proposal mentions \"hyperparameters, dataset hashes, and runtime events\" but doesn't explicitly map to Article 12(2)(a), (b), (c) — i.e., it doesn't specifically mention logging for risk identification, post-market monitoring, or operational monitoring. However, a well-designed framework would capture these implicitly through \"system events and state changes.\"\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric ID), the specific minimum requirements in Article 12(3) are not explicitly addressed — no mention of start/end timestamps per use, reference database references, input data for matches, or verification person IDs. This is a gap if the lab develops such systems.\n- \"Over the lifetime\" — the proposal says \"over the lifetime of the system\" implicitly through \"automatically captures... over the lifetime.\" Actually it doesn't explicitly say lifetime. It says \"for all high-risk models\" and mentions training and inference pipelines. The lifetime aspect (including decommissioning, archival) isn't explicitly stated but is implied.\n- \"Seamless backend utility\" and \"minimizes manual reporting burden\" — from a compliance standpoint, this is good because it reduces human error in logging, which is a major audit failure point.\n\n**My professional assessment:**\nThis is strongly aligned with Article 12's core mandate. The automatic nature addresses the \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording\" language. The tamper-evident format supports traceability and audit. Standardization across teams ensures no team falls through compliance cr