a standardized approach reduces the risk that individual projects miss logging requirements.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level - \"lightweight, plug-and-play module\" doesn't specify whether it will actually capture all the event types required by Article 12(2)(a)-(c). A truly \"lightweight\" solution might not be comprehensive enough.\n- The framing emphasizes \"maximizing scientific throughput and resource efficiency\" rather than compliance - this is a researcher-centric motivation, not a compliance-centric one. As Compliance Officer, I care that the primary design driver is regulatory adequacy, not convenience.\n- \"Standardized API hooks\" sounds good but doesn't address whether the framework can be adapted for different system types (e.g., Annex III 1(a) biometric systems need very specific logging per Art 12(3)).\n- The proposal mentions \"training and inference pipelines\" but Article 12 covers \"over the lifetime of the system\" which includes post-market monitoring, deployment changes, substantial modifications - not just training and inference.\n- No mention of log retention, integrity, immutability, or access controls - all important for demonstrating compliance to authorities.\n- The proposal doesn't address how it ensures logs are available for regulatory inspection (Article 12 is tied to the broader documentation regime).\n\n**Decision:**\nThis is a fundamentally sound direction that aligns well with Article 12's requirements. A unified, automated approach to logging across all high-risk systems is exactly what the regulation contemplates (\"technically allow for the automatic recording of events\"). It reduces compliance risk institutionally and supports market reputation. However, as written, it's somewhat vague on scope (lifetime vs. just training/inference) and the motivation framing is off for a compliance function. That said, the core concept - institutionalized automated logging infrastructure - is strongly aligned with what Article 12 demands. I would approve this because the fundamental approach (standardized, automatic, across all projects) directly serves compliance objectives, even though implementation details need to be tightened.\n\nPromise score: This is a strong direction. From my vantage point, it addresses the core requirement of Art 12(1) and supports Art 12(2). The fact that it's institution-wide rather than per-project significantly reduces compliance risk. I'd say around 78-82. It's not a perfect proposal (scope gaps, motivation framing), but the fundamental architecture is right. I'll go with 80.\n",
