is marketing language, not a technical plan. It doesn't specify what existing infrastructure would be leveraged, what the delta is from current logging capabilities, or how it integrates with our existing monitoring systems (Article 72 post-market monitoring).\n- The scope is vague: \"create human-comprehensible logs\" and \"develop tools to translate complex system logs into readable reports\" — this could range from a small dashboard project to a massive NLP/explainability research program. Without specificity, I can't assess resource requirements.\n- From an efficiency standpoint, if we already have logging infrastructure in place (which any serious AI lab developing high-risk systems would need for basic debugging and monitoring), the incremental work of making those logs \"human-comprehensible\" or adding anomaly detection highlighting might be a modest engineering effort rather than a \"specialized research initiative.\"\n- The proposal doesn't address how it connects to existing R&D infrastructure. It reads as if proposing something from scratch.\n\n**Decision reasoning:**\nThe underlying need is real (compliance requires traceable, understandable logs), but the proposal as framed is too vague and resource-heavy for my approval bar. It positions a compliance engineering task as an open research project without specifying how it leverages existing infrastructure or what specific gap it fills beyond basic logging. The \"competitive market advantage\" framing further suggests it's more of a strategic marketing narrative than a focused implementation plan. I would want to see this reframed as: (1) audit our existing logging, (2) identify specific gaps against Article 12(2), (3) allocate targeted engineering resources to close those gaps using existing infrastructure.\n\nHowever, the core idea — making logs actionable for compliance and risk identification — is sound and aligned with regulatory requirements. It's not a bad idea per se; it's poorly scoped and resou