ate in the development pipeline. For an R&D lab focused on speed and agility, this is a bureaucracy layer that adds lead time before we can even start building.\n- The framing of \"protecting our scientific agility\" is somewhat misleading — it's really about avoiding unnecessary compliance overhead, which is fair, but the mechanism (a review board) is process-heavy.\n- It doesn't address any technical implementation question: What logging architecture? What event granularity? How to make logs tamper-evident? What storage requirements? These are the questions I care about as a dev engineer.\n- The proposal essentially says \"let's figure out if we need to comply before we build\" — which is obvious and doesn't constitute an actionable technical solution. It's a meta-process step, not a solution.\n\nFrom my vantage point: This is a governance/process proposal that I'm not the right function to evaluate. It doesn't improve technical viability or prototyping speed/quality in any concrete way. If anything, adding a review board gate could slow down prototyping cycles. However, it's not harmful — it's just not technically actionable and not within my domain of concern in a meaningful way.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Does it have ANY value from my perspective? Knowing upfront whether a project needs Article 12 logging IS useful for architecture decisions. If you know at the start that you need automatic event recording over the system's lifetime, you design differently than if you don't. So there's a kernel of practical value: \"scope your compliance requirements early so you can architect accordingly.\" But the proposal as stated is about creating a review board and classification protocol — it's an organizational process, not a technical solution.\n\nI think I'd lean toward reject from my vantage point. It's not technically actionable, it introduces process overhead that could slow prototyping, and it doesn't address any technical implementation question I care abou