f purpose limitation.\n  3. From a market reputation standpoint, framing compliance logs as material for \"scientific discovery\" could signal to regulators that we're treating our logging obligations casually—as a data mine rather than a serious regulatory obligation. This is a reputational risk with authorities.\n  4. The proposal says it \"transforms a regulatory burden into...\" language—this framing is risky from a compliance perspective. It suggests the logs are primarily a burden being repurposed, rather than a core safety mechanism. If this framing leaked or was seen by regulators, it could undermine trust.\n  5. On the positive side: better understanding of failure modes does support post-market monitoring (Art. 72) and risk management (Art. 9), which ARE compliance obligations. So there's a legitimate compliance angle here—if the \"forensic analysis\" feeds back into our post-market surveillance processes, it directly supports Art. 12(2)(b).\n  6. However, the proposal as stated frames this primarily as \"scientific discovery\" and improving \"intellectual merit of our safety research\"—not as a compliance function. The intent and framing matter to regulators.\n\n**Decision:**\nThe core issue: this proposal's primary purpose is scientific research/intellectual pursuit using compliance data, not regulatory compliance per se. While it could incidentally support post-market monitoring, the stated aim (\"turn compliance data into scientific discovery,\" \"improving the intellectual merit of our safety research\") misaligns with my mandate. From a compliance standpoint:\n- It creates data governance risks (purpose limitation, especially for personal data in logs)\n- The framing is reputationally risky with regulators\n- It doesn't directly serve a compliance obligation as its primary purpose\n- It could create confusion about what the logs are \"for\" if audited\n\nHowever, there IS a legitimate compliance kernel here: analyzing logs to identify failure pattern