        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1275\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core instinct of a shared logging API is sound, but framing it as a single 'universal' library with one rigid schema that must be integrated into every deployment pipeline creates a hard coupling that will slow prototyping: R&D teams iterate on heterogeneous stacks (different serving runtimes, edge vs. cloud inference, novel architectures still under test), and a locked shared schema forces rework each time the event model needs to evolve during experimentation. The 'absolute consistency' guarantee is also an overclaim — Article 12(2) requires logging be calibrated to each system's intended purpose, which in practice means per-domain event semantics that a one-size-fits-all library will handle poorly.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 38\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen proposal P1275 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution whose domain of concern is \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\"\n\nLet me analyze this proposal through that lens:\n\n**Proposal P1275: Universal High-Risk Logging Framework**\n\nThe idea: A centralized software library providing standardized event logging across all high-risk AI projects. Standardized time-stamping, metadata capture, shared schema, engineers integrate API into deployment pipelines for automatic recording of operational events and risk triggers.\n\n**From my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\n\nPositives:\n- A shared library/API approach is technically sound in principle — it avoids each team reinventing logging wheels.\n- Standardized schema does help with auditability and reduces integration friction downstream.\n- Automatic recording via API hooks into deployment pipelines aligns well with how ML infrastructure already works (training scripts, serving endpoints, etc.).\n\nConcerns from a prototyping speed/viability perspective:\n- \"Universal\" is a red flag for prototyping speed. A single framework trying to serve ALL high-risk AI projects (biometrics in Annex III 1(a), medical devices, critical infrastructure, etc.) will be over-engineered at the start. Different project types have wildly different event structures — a face recognition system logs very different events than an NLP-based document classifier used in education scoring.\n- \"Absolute consistency\" is an engineering ideal that creates rigidity. In prototyping, you often need to iterate on what's being logged before you know what matters. A locked shared schema slows down experimentation.\n- The proposal says \"engineers will integrate this API into every model deployment pipeline\" — this assumes a uniform deployment architecture across all projects, which is rarely true in an R&D lab where teams experiment with different stacks (JAX vs PyTorch, different serving frameworks, edge vs cloud deployments).\n- \"Centralized software library\" introduces a single point of dependency and a maintenance bottleneck. If the logging library has a bug or needs an update, it blocks all teams. For prototyping speed, this is a significant drag.\n- The proposal doesn't address how to handle the specific Article 12(3) requirements (start/end dates, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification) — these are domain-specific and a \"universal\" framework may not cleanly map.\n- \"Guarantees baseline compliance\" is an overclaim from an engineering perspective. A logging library records events; it doesn't guarantee the system presents no risk or that all relevant events are captured for the specific intended purpose (Article 12(2) requires logging be \"appropriate to the intended purpose\").\n\nOverall assessment: The core idea of a shared logging API is technically viable and useful, but the framing as a \"universal\" framework with \"absolute consistency\" and a single shared schema that must be integrated into ALL pipelines is over-constrained for an R&D environment where prototyping speed matters. It will slow down teams who need to iterate quickly on novel architectures. The proposal reads more like a compliance infrastructure play than a development-engineering one.\n\nI'd lean toward rejecting this from my vantage point because:\n1. The \"universal\" scope and rigid shared schema conflict with prototyping agility.\n2. It creates a single dependency that could bottleneck multiple teams.\n3. It assumes uniformity in deployment pipelines that doesn't exist in R&D.\n4. From a technical viability standpoint, it's more ambitious than necessary — a lighter-weight approach (per-project logging adapters to a common schema, or even just well-documented patterns) would achieve 80% of the benefit with far less upfront investment and much less coupling.\n\nHowever, I should note there IS value in standardization. The promise score should reflect that it's not garbage — the core instinct is right but the execution as proposed would hurt prototyping speed. Maybe a 35-45? It's a reasonable idea executed in a way that conflicts with my domain priorities.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, with a promise around 38. The concept has merit but the \"universal\" and \"absolute consistency\" framing makes it technically over-constrained for an R&D prototyping environment. A lighter, more modular approach would serve both compliance and speed better.\n",
