rmance), but the proposal frames it as open-ended research into well-known techniques rather than as a concrete engineering task with deliverables. From my speed/prototyping vantage point, this is too diffuse and slow to be useful. The actual engineering work needed is straightforward architecture design and implementation of async logging with appropriate buffering — not \"research into the efficiency frontier.\"\n\nPromise score: I'd give it maybe 30-40. The underlying problem is real and worth addressing, but the framing as open research makes it a poor use of engineering resources when a targeted architecture sprint would achieve the same goal faster. It's not terrible (the techniques mentioned are relevant), but it's misframed for our needs.\n\nLet me settle on 35 — there's legitimate technical content here, and if the system is truly latency-critical, some optimization work beyond \"just use async logging\" might be warranted. But as proposed, it's too open-ended and research-flavored for my function's priorities.\n",
