nce pressure\n6. As a mandatory gate, it creates an institutional record that compliance was verified - useful for demonstrating due diligence to regulators (traceability of our own compliance processes)\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n1. \"Simulate high-risk events\" - the adequacy of simulation is a question. Can you truly simulate all edge cases? A compliance officer would want assurance that the test coverage is comprehensive, not just spot-checking. But this is an inherent limitation of any testing framework, not unique to this proposal.\n2. The proposal focuses on Article 12 specifically (record-keeping). It doesn't mention how logs are stored, protected, retained (which falls under other articles), but that's beyond scope - it's focused on whether the logging capability exists and works.\n3. \"Mandatory quality gate\" - this is good for compliance enforcement within the institution. A non-compliance officer would worry about exceptions being granted. But as written, it says \"mandatory,\" which is what I want to hear.\n\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):\n- This directly protects against regulatory non-compliance on a \"shall\" requirement - failure here means the system is simply non-compliant and cannot be lawfully placed on the EU market\n- It creates an institutional process that demonstrates active compliance management to regulators during audits or market surveillance\n- It reduces the risk of shipping a product that fails post-market monitoring requirements (Article 72) because logs weren't properly configured\n- It protects market reputation by preventing publicized compliance failures\n- The CI integration means it scales across all high-risk systems without proportional headcount increase\n\nThis is a strong proposal from my perspective. It directly operationalizes a mandatory regulatory requirement into an enforceable institutional process. I would approve this with high confidence.\n\nPromise score