ecital 71's requirement for documentation on training, testing processes.\n- \"Seamless audit trail from hypothesis to deployment\" — supports traceability which is explicitly mentioned in Recital 71.\n- \"Middleware layer integrated into our training pipelines to log telemetry without requiring manual researcher input\" — this addresses the automatic nature required by Art 12(1). Removing human dependency for logging reduces risk of non-compliance due to forgotten/incomplete manual logs.\n- \"Version-controlled snapshots of all inputs\" — supports the requirement for documentation on data used (Recital 71).\n\n**Weaknesses/Gaps from a compliance perspective:**\n- The proposal focuses heavily on *development* phase (\"during model development,\" \"training pipelines\"). Article 12(1) requires logging \"over the lifetime of the system\" — this includes post-deployment operation. The proposal mentions \"hypothesis to deployment\" but doesn't clearly address ongoing operational logging post-deployment, which is critical for Art 12(2)(a)-(c).\n- Art 12(2) specifically requires logging events relevant to identifying risk situations (Art 79(1)), facilitating post-market monitoring (Art 72), and monitoring operation (Art 26(5)). The proposal talks about \"telemetry\" but doesn't explicitly address these specific event categories.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems, Art 12(3) has very specific requirements (start/end times of each use, reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons in verification). The proposal doesn't mention addressing these specific minimums.\n- \"Intellectual merit of our publications through perfect reproducibility\" — while this is a nice secondary benefit, it's not a compliance driver. However, reproducibility does support the traceability requirement in Recital 71.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- Building middleware into training pipelines is a significant infrastructure investment. If the lab has mul