ng existing experiment tracking tools means leveraging what's already there rather than building from scratch — pragmatic and cost-effective\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n- The proposal focuses on \"technical documentation\" and \"logs\" but doesn't explicitly address the specific minimum logging requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric identification). It's somewhat generic.\n- \"Compliance-ready reports\" is a bit vague — compliance with what exactly? The full Article 12 framework includes multiple specific data points that must be captured.\n- \"Custom scripts to export\" raises questions about robustness, auditability of the system itself, and whether it can withstand regulatory scrutiny. A \"custom script\" approach might be fragile.\n- It doesn't mention the logging for \"identifying situations that may result in a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1)\" (Article 12(2)(a)) or post-market monitoring events (12(2)(b)).\n- The proposal is framed as reducing \"administrative overhead for the engineering team\" — this is an efficiency framing rather than a compliance-risk framing. From my vantage point, I care about whether it actually meets the regulatory requirements, not whether it saves engineers time. However, reducing manual error IS a compliance benefit.\n- Doesn't mention who is accountable for verifying the auto-generated documentation is complete and accurate. Compliance requires someone to sign off.\n- \"Real-time\" generation sounds good but doesn't address retention periods or access controls that regulators may need.\n\n**Institutional survival / market reputation angle:**\n- Having automated, consistent record-keeping is a significant reputational asset — shows the institution takes compliance seriously\n- If a regulator comes to audit, having a systematic, version-controlled trail is far better than scattered manual documents\n- However, if the system produces incomplete or inaccurate \"compliance-ready\" documentation automatically, that could be worse than no automation at all (false sense of security)\n\n**Decision:**\nThis proposal addresses the core spirit and letter of Article 12 well. It's a strong structural response to the automatic recording requirement. The integration with existing tools makes it feasible without massive new investment. However, it needs significant elaboration to cover the specific minimum requirements in Article 12(3), the event categories in Article 12(2)(a)-(c), and accountability for verification of auto-generated content. As written, it's a solid foundation but not a complete compliance solution.\n\nFrom my vantage point: this is genuinely useful for institutional survival (compliance readiness) and market reputation (demonstrating systematic approach). It reduces the risk of missing records or having inconsistent documentation. I'd approve it as a direction to pursue, though implementation would need careful scoping against each specific requirement in Article 12.\n\nPromise score: I think this is quite strong — maybe 78-82. It addresses the core requirement directly, leverages existing infrastructure, and reduces compliance risk through automation. The deductions are for its generality (doesn't map to specific sub-requirements), the \"custom scripts\" fragility concern, and the lack of accountability/verification framework built in.\n\nI'll go with 80.\n",
