work means one audit finding doesn't cascade into systemic non-compliance across all projects. It protects market reputation by showing regulators we have institutionalized compliance.\n\nThe gaps I noted (incomplete enumeration of Art 12(3) elements, lifetime persistence, post-market monitoring content) are implementation details that the engineering team would need to address in building out the framework. The proposal as a strategic direction is sound and directly addresses the regulatory requirement.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score. It's not perfect (the description could be more comprehensive regarding all Art 12 sub-requirements), but it hits the core compliance need squarely and provides institutional control that protects against non-compliance.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 82-85. It's strongly aligned with my mandate, addresses a direct regulatory requirement, and creates an institutional safeguard. The slight deduction is because the proposal as stated doesn't explicitly cover all of Art 12(3)'s minimum elements or the lifetime persistence/retention aspects, and it's purely technical without mentioning governance overlays that I'd want alongside it.\n",
