ory requirements as structured rules that can be matched against technical artifacts. This is doable but the maintenance burden as regulations evolve (or as your systems change architecture) could be significant.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A prototype for the version-control-parsing + basic metadata extraction part is quite fast - maybe a few weeks.\n- But a genuinely useful system that covers the breadth of Recital 71 requirements would require deep domain knowledge of both the regulatory text AND the specific technical architecture of each AI system. This makes it hard to build a generic, reusable prototype quickly.\n- The ROI for prototyping: if you can't demonstrate clear value in a short sprint (say 2-4 weeks), it's hard to justify committing scarce implementation resources.\n- There's a chicken-and-egg problem: the tool depends on good structured logging existing first (which is itself Article 12(1) compliance work). If your logs aren't well-structured, this tool has nothing meaningful to parse.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe idea has some merit as an auxiliary tool - automating the extraction of certain metadata from version control and structured logs into a documentation scaffold could save time. However:\n1. The technical viability is moderate-to-low for the full scope described. Much of what Recital 71 requires cannot be \"automatically populated\" from code repos and logs - it requires engineering narrative, risk analysis, design decisions that aren't captured in version control.\n2. The prototyping speed concern: a meaningful prototype would require first having structured logging infrastructure in place, plus encoding regulatory mapping rules, plus understanding the specific system architecture. This is not a quick win.\n3. The \"mapping to official templates\" step assumes a level of template rigidity that may not exist or may change as guidance evolves.\n4. However, a narrower version (auto-generating sections about model version, training d