n help.\n- The risk: building this middleware itself is a significant engineering investment. Before it's done, it provides zero value. And once built, maintaining it across evolving research stacks is ongoing cost. If the compliance requirements evolve (and they likely will as implementing guidance comes), the middleware needs updates too.\n\n**Article 12 Specifics:**\n- The proposal captures \"timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs\" - this maps reasonably to Article 12(3)(a) start/end dates, 12(3)(b) reference database (input hashes could serve as proof), and 12(3)(d) identification of natural persons.\n- However, it's somewhat thin on the specifics: \"the reference database against which input data has been checked\" is not just a hash - you need to record WHICH database version was used. \"The input data for which the search has led to a match\" needs more than a hash - or at least a verifiable reference. The proposal says \"input hashes\" which might be sufficient for traceability but could be insufficient for actual compliance demonstration depending on what regulators expect.\n- For Article 12(2)(a) (identifying risk situations), 12(2)(b) (post-market monitoring), 12(2)(c) (monitoring operation per Art 26(5)) - the proposal is vague. It says \"automate event recording\" but doesn't specify how it captures the *relevant* events for these purposes, which are system-specific.\n\n**My Assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound and aligns with what I'd recommend: don't make every research team reinvent compliance logging from scratch. A shared infrastructure layer makes sense. The technical approach (API + storage in compute cluster) is viable.\n\nHowever, the execution risks concern me from a prototyping-speed perspective:\n1. Building this is itself a large project that will consume engineering resources for months before delivering value.\n2. \"Baked into base images\" is a strong commitment that could slow down experiments if it conflicts with research needs.\n3. 