ents conflict\n- Even if the specific technical solutions (hashing, enclaves) turn out not to satisfy regulators, the research will clarify what IS acceptable\n\nBut wait - is this really a \"compliance\" project or an \"R&D\" project? It's framed as R&D. From my vantage point, I need to ask: does this get us to compliance, or does it keep us in uncertainty? \n\nThe proposal says \"Research Project\" and \"develop logging mechanisms.\" This is forward-looking. But the current obligation under Art 12(3)(c) seems to require actual input data recording. If we can't satisfy that NOW, we're already non-compliant for biometric systems regardless of what future R&D produces.\n\nHowever, I should note: \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" could be interpreted as requiring identification of WHICH data matched, not necessarily storing all raw data in perpetuity. There's interpretive room. Understanding that room IS compliance work.\n\nMy decision: This is a legitimate and important project from my vantage point. The GDPR/AI Act tension for biometric logging is one of the most critical compliance challenges we face. If unresolved, it threatens our ability to operate at all (institutional survival) or exposes us to dual enforcement (market reputation). I would approve this with some caution about timelines and interim compliance measures.\n\nPromise score: This addresses a real and serious compliance gap. But there's uncertainty about whether the technical solutions proposed will be accepted by regulators, and it's research rather than implementation. I'd put this around 72-78. It's important but not guaranteed to resolve the issue cleanly. Let me go with 75.\n",
