tor role is a fixed cost but predictable.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:** A secure repository for documentation could leverage existing LIMS (Laboratory Information Management Systems) or document management systems already in place, rather than building something new. The standardized template approach means the process scales across projects without proportional resource growth. However, if we don't have such a system, this represents new infrastructure investment.\n\n**Assessment:**\n- The proposal is fundamentally sound - it addresses a real regulatory requirement (Recital 71) in a structured way.\n- From my vantage point, the key concern is the \"compliance gate\" mechanism. Making documentation a hard blocker at every milestone could create bottlenecks. However, without such a gate, compliance becomes an afterthought and the eventual audit preparation cost balloons dramatically.\n- The standardized template is a force multiplier - it reduces per-project documentation effort significantly once established.\n- The \"dedicated auditor\" is a real resource cost but a predictable one. In my experience, ad-hoc compliance verification by overburdened project leads is far less efficient and more error-prone.\n- The proposal doesn't specify whether the secure repository would be built new or leverage existing infrastructure - this is an implementation detail that could go either way on cost.\n\nOverall: This is a reasonable, structured approach to a genuine regulatory requirement. From my resource-allocation perspective, it front-loads some fixed costs (template development, auditor hiring) but reduces variable per-milestone costs and prevents the much larger expense of non-compliance or rushed audit preparation. The main risk is the gate mechanism becoming a bottleneck if not well-designed, but that's an implementation detail rather than a fundamental flaw in the proposal.\n\nI'd lean toward approve with a moderate-to-high promise score. It's good operational thinking, addresses real needs efficiently (standardization reduces marginal cost), and the auditor role, while a fixed cost, is more efficient than distributed ad-hoc compliance work. The gate mechanism has some risk of slowing scientific throughput but is necessary for the system to actually work.\n\nI'll approve it with a promise score around 72-75. It's solid operational infrastructure that I'd back, though I'd want to see the implementation details on how the gate won't become a bottleneck and whether we can leverage existing document management systems.\n",
