cific data points (period of use, reference database, input data leading to match, identification of natural persons in verification). The proposal mentions \"input data\" generically but doesn't explicitly call out all the Article 12(3) specifics. However, this is a design detail that would be addressed during implementation.\n- The scope says \"all high-risk projects\" — this is good because it ensures no project falls through the cracks.\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps and input data\" which covers part of what's needed but doesn't explicitly mention all elements (e.g., reference databases, identification of verifiers). Again, a design detail.\n- From a compliance perspective, having a SINGLE framework is actually better than piecemeal solutions because it creates accountability and consistency.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) — automatic logging over lifetime. It promotes uniformity which reduces compliance risk across multiple projects. The centralized nature means one team owns the implementation, making it easier to verify that all systems are compliant. The standardized API approach means engineers can't easily skip or modify the logging in ways that create gaps.\n\nThe main risk is scope — will it truly cover all the specific requirements of Article 12(2)(a)-(c) and 12(3)(a)-(d)? But as a framework proposal, this is exactly the right architecture to ensure those can be embedded.\n\nFrom institutional survival: non-compliance with Article 12 could lead to fines (up to €7.5M or 1% of global turnover for record-keeping violations under Article 99). A unified approach minimizes the chance that any one system falls short.\n\nFrom market reputation: being able to demonstrate a systematic, lab-wide compliance infrastructure for logging is far more credible to regulators and market partners than ad-hoc per-project solutions.\n\nI would approve this with high confidence. The promise score should be high — maybe 88-92. It's not a perfect 100 because the proposal as stated doesn't explicitly map to all the granular requirements of Article 12(3), and there's an implementation risk that the \"standardized API\" might not capture everything needed for every use case. But architecturally, it's exactly what compliance needs.\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 90.\n",
