his becomes a 2-3 year research project when a more pragmatic engineering solution could meet compliance in 6 months.\n\nHowever, I can't say it's worthless. The privacy angle IS relevant — if we're logging personal data as part of verification records, we do need to handle that carefully under GDPR alongside the AI Act. A lightweight version of this (e.g., using pseudonymization or hashing for the specific fields required by Art 12(3)(c)) could be a practical engineering task within existing infrastructure rather than a \"dedicated R&D project.\"\n\nThe proposal as written, though, is framed as a research exploration (\"investigate,\" \"aim to create\"), which from my resource-allocation perspective is riskier and more expensive than an engineering implementation. I'm torn — it has merit but the framing makes it hard to approve without seeing tighter scoping.\n\nI'll lean toward reject because:\n- The scope is too open-ended for a dedicated R&D commitment\n- It likely over-engineers the compliance need\n- It introduces infrastructure dependencies (secure enclaves) not in our current setup\n- A simpler engineering approach would meet Article 12 requirements without this level of investment\n- The \"competitive advantage\" framing suggests it's motivated by marketing rather than actual operational need\n\nBut I'll give it a moderate promise score because the underlying problem (privacy in audit logs) is real and worth at least a lightweight investigation.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, with a promise around 40-50. It's not nothing — the problem is legitimate — but as framed, it's too resource-intensive and under-specified for my approval bar given funding constraints. I'll go with 42.\n",
