ith \"attribution techniques and pointer mechanisms within the model architecture.\" This sounds like fundamental research — developing new architectural methods for data attribution. That's expensive, uncertain in outcome, and potentially far from what Article 12 actually requires. Article 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems is quite specific: record the reference database used, input data that matched, period of use, and identification of verifying persons. This is more of an engineering/logging task than a deep interpretability research project.\n- **Scientific ambition vs. funding constraints**: The proposal conflates two very different things: (1) implementing compliance logging (which is relatively straightforward engineering for the specific Annex III 1(a) case), and (2) developing fundamental attribution/interpretability techniques that could map any model output to training data subsets (which is a massive, open research problem). Funding a \"research initiative\" for the latter under the guise of regulatory compliance is a mismatch. The lab's scarce implementation resources would be better spent on straightforward compliance engineering if that's what's actually needed.\n- **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: If the lab already has interpretability researchers, this might leverage existing expertise. But \"pointer mechanisms within the model architecture\" suggests modifying core models, which is a different resource pool than logging/monitoring infrastructure. This could fragment efforts.\n- The proposal seems to over-scope: Article 12's requirements are well-defined and specific (especially paragraph 3 for Annex III 1(a)). You don't need a \"research initiative\" with \"attribution techniques and pointer mechanisms\" to record start/end times of use, reference databases used, matched input data, and verifier IDs. That's logging infrastructure, not interpretability research.\n- However, there IS value in the broader interpretation: if you can build attribution capabilities that go beyond what the regulation strictly requires, that could serve multiple purposes (compliance + scientific advancement). But the risk is that the lab commits scarce resources to an open-ended research project when a more targeted engineering effort would satisfy the regulatory requirement at lower cost and with less uncertainty.\n\nMy assessment: The proposal has a noble framing but suffers from significant scope inflation from my resource-allocation perspective. It risks committing scarce R&D resources to a fundamental research problem (model-level attribution) under the banner of compliance, when the actual regulatory requirement is more narrowly defined and achievable through engineering. The \"scientific opportunity\" framing is attractive but could be a trap that diverts implementation resources away from what's actually needed for compliance. I'd lean toward reject because the resource commitment implied by a \"research initiative\" with architectural modifications is disproportionate to the actual regulatory need, and the outcome is uncertain (attribution techniques are not solved).\n\nHowever, there's some merit — if the lab already has interpretability researchers whose work could be redirected or leveraged, and if the broader capability would serve multiple compliance requirements across different high-risk systems... but as stated, it reads like a greenfield research commitment.\n\nI'll reject with a moderate promise score (maybe 35-45) — there's genuine scientific value in the direction, but from my vantage point of resource allocation and meeting regulatory obligations efficiently, this is over-scoped and under-specified for what Article 12 actually demands.\n",
