        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1936 suggests creating a specialized cross-functional support team (two dedicated compliance engineers) to handle Article 12 logging infrastructure across all R&D projects, removing that burden from primary scientific researchers.\n\nLet me think about this from my professional vantage point:\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- The idea is organizationally sound in principle - having dedicated people who understand the technical requirements of Article 12 (logging capabilities, automatic event recording, traceability) could mean more consistent implementation across projects.\n- However, \"two dedicated compliance engineers\" for a lab that presumably has many R&D pods and multiple high-risk AI systems seems like it might be under-resourced. Each system may need different logging architectures depending on its intended purpose (Article 12(2)), and Annex III point 1(a) systems have specific minimum requirements.\n- The technical challenge here is that Article 12 requires \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events\" - this is deeply embedded in the architecture of each system. A compliance engineer acting as a \"consultant\" may not have deep enough context into each pod's specific architecture to implement proper logging. They'd need to be embedded, not just consulting.\n- The speed/quality concern: Having two people as consultants across multiple pods means they become a bottleneck. Each pod would need to wait for their availability. This could actually SLOW down prototyping rather than help it, if the compliance engineers are the gatekeepers for logging implementation.\n\n**Speed of prototyping:**\n- On one hand, removing this from researchers' plates could speed up their core work.\n- On the other hand, if every pod has to wait for 2 shared engineers to implement or review their logging, that creates a serialization bottleneck. Prototyping cycles get longer because you can't move forward until the compliance engineer reviews/implements your logging layer.\n- The \"consultant\" model is particularly problematic for speed - consultants come and go, don't maintain deep context, and create dependency points.\n\n**Quality:**\n- Specialized engineers who focus on this might build better, more consistent logging infrastructure.\n- But the risk is that they become a compliance checkbox function rather than being deeply integrated into the technical architecture. Proper logging (especially for Annex III 1(a) systems with specific requirements like reference database tracking, input data matching records, etc.) requires deep integration with the system's actual data flows and decision logic.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe organizational intuition is correct - you don't want every researcher figuring out logging from scratch. But the specific mechanism (2 shared consultants) has significant technical viability concerns:\n1. Bottleneck risk for prototyping speed\n2. \"Consultant\" model may not provide deep enough architectural integration\n3. Two people may be insufficient depending on scale\n4. The logging requirements are deeply tied to system architecture - a separate team risks building bolt-on solutions rather than architecturally integrated ones\n\nHowever, the core idea of having dedicated technical expertise for this domain does have merit. The issue is more about execution model (embedded vs. consultant) and resource sizing.\n\nI think this is a mixed proposal. The organizational principle is sound but the specific implementation has technical viability concerns from my perspective. I'd lean toward rejecting or giving it a low-to-mid promise score because the \"two consultants for multiple pods\" model creates architectural and speed problems that could actually hurt prototyping velocity rather than help it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says they \"handle the technical implementation of logging capabilities for each high-risk system.\" If these two people are deeply embedded (even if called consultants), and they build reusable infrastructure/frameworks that pods plug into, that could actually work well. A shared logging framework with pod-specific configuration could be efficient.\n\nBut the risk remains: 2 people as a bottleneck, consultant model creating dependency, and the fundamental challenge that proper Article 12 compliance requires understanding each system's specific architecture deeply enough to know WHAT events are relevant (Article 12(2) is purpose-specific).\n\nI'll reject this one. The core technical concern from my vantage point: the \"two consultants for multiple pods\" model creates a serialization bottleneck that directly conflicts with prototyping speed, and the consultant separation risks shallow architectural integration rather than the deep system-level logging that Article 12(1) requires (\"technically allow for automatic recording\"). Better approaches would be to embed compliance engineering within each pod or build shared infrastructure/frameworks that pods implement themselves.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 30-35. The organizational insight has some value but the specific mechanism undermines the speed/quality goals I care about.\n",
