onsuming if done naively. How many systems do we have? Is this a one-time cost or recurring?\n- The proposal is somewhat vague on scope: \"categorize systems by risk level\" — who does this categorization? Does it require new headcount?\n- \"Mandatory compliance checklist for every project lead\" — this adds administrative burden to research scientists. There's tension between scientific ambition and compliance overhead. However, a simple checklist is relatively low-cost compared to building out logging infrastructure.\n- Quarterly legal review — this could be a recurring cost but also provides efficiency by catching issues early before they compound.\n- The framing around \"institutional survival or market reputation\" is somewhat alarmist but the underlying point about regulatory risk is valid.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\n- This proposal is essentially a diagnostic/assessment phase before any heavy implementation investment. From a resource-efficiency standpoint, this is actually smart — you don't want to build logging infrastructure for systems that aren't high-risk, or miss critical gaps in systems that are.\n- The cost here is relatively modest (time for audit, checklist design, quarterly legal review) compared to the alternative of reactive compliance or building everything without knowing what's needed.\n- It leverages existing structures (project leads, legal team) rather than requiring new infrastructure.\n- However, \"comprehensive\" could be a resource black hole if not scoped carefully.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- This is an enabling activity — it doesn't directly advance research but protects the ability to continue research by ensuring compliance.\n- The checklist adds some overhead but is proportionate.\n- It doesn't divert significant R&D resources from scientific work.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Maps what we have against requirements — good use of existing knowledge.