d/quality of prototyping):**\nThe core idea has merit — dedicated engineering ownership for cross-cutting infrastructure like logging is generally good practice. However, the framing as separating \"scientific\" from \"administrative coding\" creates a dangerous dichotomy that could lead to poor technical integration. The proposal as stated risks creating a bottleneck or a disconnected compliance layer rather than an integrated engineering capability.\n\nI think this clears my bar with caveats. The fundamental structure (dedicated team for infrastructure) is technically sound and would likely improve quality of logging implementations. But the \"reallocate headcount\" approach without specifying how integration with research teams works is a risk to prototyping speed. I'd lean toward approving it because the core architectural decision (centralized ownership of logging/traceability infrastructure) is correct, even if the execution details need work.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"non-scientific architectural requirements\" and \"administrative coding tasks.\" This framing suggests a separation that could be technically harmful. Logging that captures \"situations that may result in risk\" (Article 12(2)(a)) requires deep understanding of model behavior, failure modes, and edge cases — this isn't purely administrative. If the team is siloed away from the research teams building the models, they'll produce generic, shallow logging that doesn't capture what actually matters for compliance.\n\nBut then again, a well-designed platform approach could work: the pool provides the framework, schemas, storage, and audit tooling; the research teams provide domain-specific event definitions. This is how good infrastructure teams operate in large engineering orgs.\n\nI'll approve it but with a moderate promise score reflecting the execution risk around integration and the somewhat reductive framing.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 62-68. The core idea i